  "contracts": {
    "lem-nrqmx-projector-bundle-kato-well-definedness": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of Choice function: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-nrqmx-smooth-projector-bundle",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $M$ be a finite-dimensional smooth manifold, let $d\\geq1$ be an integer, and give $\\mathbb C^d$ its standard Hermitian inner product. Let $P:M\\to\\operatorname{End}(\\mathbb C^d)$ be a $C^2$ map such that $P(x)^2=P(x)=P(x)^*$ and $\\operatorname{rank}P(x)=r$ is constant. Define\n$$E_P=\\{(x,v)\\in M\\times\\mathbb C^d:P(x)v=v\\},\\qquad \\pi(x,v)=x.$$\nIts fiber at $x$ is $\\operatorname{ran}P(x)$ with the inner product inherited from $\\mathbb C^d$. For a $C^1$ section $s:U\\to\\mathbb C^d$ over an open $U\\subseteq M$ satisfying $P s=s$, define the projected connection by $\\nabla s=P\\,ds$, where $d$ is the componentwise exterior derivative. For a $C^2$ path $\\gamma:[0,1]\\to M$, put $P_\\gamma(t)=P(\\gamma(t))$ and $K_\\gamma(t)=[P_\\gamma'(t),P_\\gamma(t)]$. Kato transport along $\\gamma$ is the matrix solution $W_\\gamma'(t)=K_\\gamma(t)W_\\gamma(t)$, $W_\\gamma(0)=I_d$.\n\nThe connection and Kato transport are defined from the projector family itself; no Schrödinger Hamiltonian or physical time evolution is needed. A coherent uniformly gapped spectral projection of a $C^2$ finite Hermitian family is one source of such a $C^2$ family: under Countable Choice, an earlier finite-dimensional adiabatic result supplies that regularity.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "supplier_excerpts": [
        {
          "supplier": "def-countable-choice",
          "carrier": "items/def-countable-choice.md",
          "status": "Published mathematical supplier; exact current Definition inspected.",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of Choice function: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets has a choice function.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[A1] [A1] Countable Choice is the principle in [[def-countable-choice]]. It is retained from the supplied definition's gapped-spectral specialization; this local finite-dimensional construction uses no choice from an infinite family.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Definition item; no proof is claimed or required for the defining interface.",
          "consumer_use": "[A1] [A1] Countable Choice is the principle in [[def-countable-choice]]. It is retained from the supplied definition's gapped-spectral specialization; this local finite-dimensional construction uses no choice from an infinite family."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-nrqmx-smooth-projector-bundle",
          "carrier": "items/def-nrqmx-smooth-projector-bundle.md",
          "status": "Current Step 1 draft in the assigned batch; exact current carrier inspected.",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Let $M$ be a finite-dimensional smooth manifold, let $d\\geq1$ be an integer, and give $\\mathbb C^d$ its standard Hermitian inner product. Let $P:M\\to\\operatorname{End}(\\mathbb C^d)$ be a $C^2$ map such that $P(x)^2=P(x)=P(x)^*$ and $\\operatorname{rank}P(x)=r$ is constant. Define\n$$E_P=\\{(x,v)\\in M\\times\\mathbb C^d:P(x)v=v\\},\\qquad \\pi(x,v)=x.$$\nIts fiber at $x$ is $\\operatorname{ran}P(x)$ with the inner product inherited from $\\mathbb C^d$. For a $C^1$ section $s:U\\to\\mathbb C^d$ over an open $U\\subseteq M$ satisfying $P s=s$, define the projected connection by $\\nabla s=P\\,ds$, where $d$ is the componentwise exterior derivative. For a $C^2$ path $\\gamma:[0,1]\\to M$, put $P_\\gamma(t)=P(\\gamma(t))$ and $K_\\gamma(t)=[P_\\gamma'(t),P_\\gamma(t)]$. Kato transport along $\\gamma$ is the matrix solution $W_\\gamma'(t)=K_\\gamma(t)W_\\gamma(t)$, $W_\\gamma(0)=I_d$.\n\nThe connection and Kato transport are defined from the projector family itself; no Schrödinger Hamiltonian or physical time evolution is needed. A coherent uniformly gapped spectral projection of a $C^2$ finite Hermitian family is one source of such a $C^2$ family: under Countable Choice, an earlier finite-dimensional adiabatic result supplies that regularity.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[F1] [F1] [[def-nrqmx-smooth-projector-bundle]]: $P^2=P=P^*$, its rank is constant, $E_{P,x}=\\operatorname{ran}P(x)$, $\\nabla s=P\\,ds$, and the Kato equation uses $K=[P_\\gamma',P_\\gamma]$.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Current Step 1 definition; exact interface and current justified_by argument inspected; independent acceptance is not inferred.",
          "consumer_use": "[F1] [F1] [[def-nrqmx-smooth-projector-bundle]]: $P^2=P=P^*$, its rank is constant, $E_{P,x}=\\operatorname{ran}P(x)$, $\\nabla s=P\\,ds$, and the Kato equation uses $K=[P_\\gamma',P_\\gamma]$."
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Fix $x_0\\in M$ and choose an orthonormal basis $e_1,\\ldots,e_r$ of $\\operatorname{ran}P(x_0)$. The vectors $P(x)e_j$ depend $C^2$ on $x$. Their Gram matrix is the identity at $x_0$, so its determinant remains positive on a neighborhood of $x_0$; hence the vectors remain linearly independent there. Applying the finite Gram–Schmidt formulas uses only sums, products, division by positive norms, and square roots of positive functions, so it gives a $C^2$ orthonormal frame for $\\operatorname{ran}P(x)$ on that neighborhood. The maps $(x,z)\\mapsto(x,\\sum_j z_j v_j(x))$ are therefore $C^2$ local bundle charts. For $r=0$ the bundle is the zero bundle, and for $r=d$ every $P(x)=I_d$ and the bundle is trivial.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "A1",
            "construct",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "Along $\\gamma$, abbreviate $P_\\gamma$ by $P$. Differentiating $P^2=P$ gives $P'P+PP'=P'$ and $PP'P=0$. Therefore $K=P'P-PP'$ is $C^1$, $K^*=-K$, and $[K,P]=P'P+PP'=P'$. Let $M_K=\\sup_{t\\in[0,1]}\\|K(t)\\|$, set $V_0(t)=I_d$, and recursively set $V_{n+1}(t)=\\int_0^tK(u)V_n(u)\\,du$. Induction gives $\\|V_n(t)\\|\\leq(M_Kt)^n/n!$, so $W=\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty V_n$ converges uniformly and satisfies $W(t)=I_d+\\int_0^tK(u)W(u)\\,du$. The fundamental theorem of calculus gives $W'=KW$; since $K\\in C^1$, also $W\\in C^2$. If $Z$ is the difference of two solutions, iteration of $Z(t)=\\int_0^tK(u)Z(u)\\,du$ gives $\\|Z(t)\\|\\leq\\sup_{[0,1]}\\|Z\\|(M_Kt)^n/n!$ for every $n$, whose right side tends to zero, so $Z=0$.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "construct",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "For a $C^1$ scalar function $f$ and a $C^1$ section $s$ with $Ps=s$, the product rule gives $\\nabla(fs)=P\\,d(fs)=P(df\\,s+f\\,ds)=df\\,s+f\\nabla s$, so $\\nabla$ satisfies the connection Leibniz rule. For two such sections $s,t$, $d(s^*t)=(P\\,ds)^*t+s^*(P\\,dt)$ because $P=P^*$ and $Ps=s$, $Pt=t$; thus $\\nabla$ preserves the Hermitian fiber metric.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Differentiation yields $(W^*W)'=W^*(K^*+K)W=0$, hence $W^*W=I_d$; a square matrix with a left inverse is invertible, so $W$ is unitary. Also $(W^*PW)'=W^*(P'-[K,P])W=0$, so $W(t)^*P(t)W(t)=P(0)$. Multiplying by $W(t)$ on the left gives $P(t)W(t)=W(t)P(0)$, as required.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 1.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "finite_checks": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
