          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Let $\\alpha\\in\\Omega^k(M)$ and $\\beta\\in\\Omega^\\ell(M)$. Their **wedge product**\nis the differential form defined pointwise by\n\n$$ (\\alpha\\wedge\\beta)_p:=\\alpha_p\\wedge\\beta_p\\in\\operatorname{Alt}^{k+\\ell}(T_pM). $$\n\nThe next proposition verifies that this pointwise field is smooth.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[F4] [F4] [[def-wedge-product-of-differential-forms]]: the scalar wedge product is the pointwise alternating product of covectors; the displayed matrix-valued formula uses that alternating convention with ordered matrix products.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Definition item; no proof is claimed or required for the defining interface.",
          "consumer_use": "[F4] [F4] [[def-wedge-product-of-differential-forms]]: the scalar wedge product is the pointwise alternating product of covectors; the displayed matrix-valued formula uses that alternating convention with ordered matrix products."
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Differentiate $P^2=P$ in a tangent direction $X$ to obtain $(dP)(X)P+P(dP)(X)=(dP)(X)$. Multiplying on both sides by $P$ gives $P(dP)(X)P=0$. For a $C^1$ section $s$ with $Ps=s$, the curvature is $\\nabla^2s(X,Y)=P(dP(X)\\,ds(Y)-dP(Y)\\,ds(X))$. Substituting $ds(X)=dP(X)s+P\\,ds(X)$ and using $P(dP)P=0$ leaves $P(dP(X)dP(Y)-dP(Y)dP(X))Ps$. This is $P(dP\\wedge dP)P\\,s$, proving the projector formula.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "In a local frame write a section as $s=Va$. Then $\\nabla(Va)=P\\,d(Va)=V(V^*dV\\,a+da)=V(da+A a)$, where $A=V^*dV$. Differentiating $V^*V=I_r$ gives $(dV)^*V+V^*dV=0$, so $A^*=-A$. Applying $d+A$ twice gives $dA+A\\wedge A$; expressing the already computed curvature in this frame gives $F_{\\mathrm{fr}}=V^*FV=dA+A\\wedge A$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "For $V'=Vg$, direct differentiation gives $A'=V'^*dV'=g^{-1}Ag+g^{-1}dg$, since $g^*=g^{-1}$. The frame-coordinate covariant derivative changes from $d+A$ to $g^{-1}(d+A)g$ on coefficient sections. Squaring this identity gives $dA'+A'\\wedge A'=g^{-1}(dA+A\\wedge A)g$, which is the claimed curvature covariance.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "Under the declared Countable Choice convention [A1], for a path the definitions give $W'=[P',P]W$; [F2] proves that its unique solution is unitary and satisfies $P(t)W(t)=W(t)P(0)$. This is projector parallel transport and uses no Schrödinger evolution.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "given"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "finite_checks": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "The Statement is vacuous on an empty base; all local curvature identities have empty domain."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
