    "lem-nrqmx-c2-projector-chern-integrality": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> (Choice function).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of Choice function: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-smooth-manifold",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A **smooth $n$-manifold** is a pair $(M,\\mathcal S)$ in which $M$ is a\ntopological $n$-manifold\n([[def-topological-manifold-without-boundary]]) and $\\mathcal S$ is a **smooth\nstructure** on $M$: a maximal smooth atlas\n([[thm-each-smooth-atlas-is-contained-in-a-unique-maximal-smooth-atlas]]).\nBecause\n[[thm-each-smooth-atlas-is-contained-in-a-unique-maximal-smooth-atlas]] sends\nevery smooth atlas to the unique maximal atlas containing it, a smooth manifold\nis equivalently specified by a topological manifold $M$ together with any one\nsmooth atlas $\\mathcal A$, the structure being the generated $[\\mathcal A]$. A\nchart $(U,\\varphi)\\in\\mathcal S$ is called a **smooth chart** (or a **chart of\nthe smooth structure**); its domain is a **coordinate domain** and its\ncoordinate functions are **smooth coordinates** on $U$. When the structure is\nclear from context, the manifold itself is written $M$ in place of\n$(M,\\mathcal S)$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-c-r-and-smooth-maps-between-smooth-manifolds",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $M$ and $N$ be smooth manifolds, let $F:M\\to N$ be a map, let $p\\in M$ and\nlet $r\\in\\mathbb N_0\\cup\\{\\infty\\}$, where $\\mathbb N_0=\\{0,1,2,\\ldots\\}$.\nFor $m,q\\in\\mathbb N_0$ and an open set $E\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$, a map\n$g:E\\to\\mathbb R^q$ is **of class $C^r$** as follows. When $m=0$, every such\nmap is declared $C^r$. When $m\\ge1$, each component $g_l$ with $l<q$ must be\n$C^r$ in the sense of\n[[def-ck-and-multi-index-notation-in-several-variables]]; when $q=0$ this\ncomponent condition is vacuous.\nSuppose $F$ is continuous at $p$. Then $F$ is **of class $C^r$ at $p$** when\nthere are smooth charts $(U,\\varphi)$ of $M$ at $p$ and $(V,\\psi)$ of $N$ at\n$F(p)$ with $F(U)\\subseteq V$ such that the coordinate representative\n\n$$\\psi\\circ F\\circ\\varphi^{-1}:\\varphi(U)\\longrightarrow\\psi(V)$$\n\n([[def-coordinate-representation-of-a-map]]) is of class $C^r$ on a\nneighbourhood of $\\varphi(p)$ in this componentwise Euclidean sense. By\n[[lem-chart-independence-of-c-r-smoothness]], this condition is independent of\nthe chosen charts: if one such representative is $C^r$, then every one is, so\n\"some charts\" may be read as \"any charts\". A map is **$C^r$ on an open set**\n$W\\subseteq M$ when it is continuous on $W$ and $C^r$ at every point of $W$. A\nmap that is $C^r$ for every finite $r$ — equivalently $C^\\infty$ — is called\n**smooth**; the term **$C^r$ map between smooth manifolds** is reserved for the\ncase where $F$ is continuous and the representative condition holds at every\npoint of $M$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-nrqmx-smooth-projector-bundle",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $M$ be a finite-dimensional smooth manifold, let $d\\geq1$ be an integer, and give $\\mathbb C^d$ its standard Hermitian inner product. Let $P:M\\to\\operatorname{End}(\\mathbb C^d)$ be a $C^2$ map such that $P(x)^2=P(x)=P(x)^*$ and $\\operatorname{rank}P(x)=r$ is constant. Define\n$$E_P=\\{(x,v)\\in M\\times\\mathbb C^d:P(x)v=v\\},\\qquad \\pi(x,v)=x.$$\nIts fiber at $x$ is $\\operatorname{ran}P(x)$ with the inner product inherited from $\\mathbb C^d$. For a $C^1$ section $s:U\\to\\mathbb C^d$ over an open $U\\subseteq M$ satisfying $P s=s$, define the projected connection by $\\nabla s=P\\,ds$, where $d$ is the componentwise exterior derivative. For a $C^2$ path $\\gamma:[0,1]\\to M$, put $P_\\gamma(t)=P(\\gamma(t))$ and $K_\\gamma(t)=[P_\\gamma'(t),P_\\gamma(t)]$. Kato transport along $\\gamma$ is the matrix solution $W_\\gamma'(t)=K_\\gamma(t)W_\\gamma(t)$, $W_\\gamma(0)=I_d$.\n\nThe connection and Kato transport are defined from the projector family itself; no Schrödinger Hamiltonian or physical time evolution is needed. A coherent uniformly gapped spectral projection of a $C^2$ finite Hermitian family is one source of such a $C^2$ family: under Countable Choice, an earlier finite-dimensional adiabatic result supplies that regularity.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-nrqmx-berry-projector-curvature",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $M$, $d$, and $P$ satisfy [[def-nrqmx-smooth-projector-bundle]], with constant rank $r$. The curvature of the projected connection $\\nabla=P\\,d$, as an endomorphism-valued two-form on $\\mathbb C^d$, is\n$$F=P(dP\\wedge dP)P.$$\nFor any local $C^2$ orthonormal frame $V:U\\to\\mathbb C^{d\\times r}$ with $V^*V=I_r$ and $P=VV^*$, its connection matrix $A=V^*dV$ is anti-Hermitian and its curvature matrix is $F_{\\mathrm{fr}}=dA+A\\wedge A=V^*FV$. If $V'=Vg$ for a $C^2$ map $g:U\\to U(r)$, then $A'=g^{-1}Ag+g^{-1}dg$ and $F'_{\\mathrm{fr}}=g^{-1}F_{\\mathrm{fr}}g$. Along every $C^2$ path, Kato transport is unitary and intertwines the endpoint projectors.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-smooth-partitions-of-unity-exist-on-manifolds",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. Every open cover of a smooth manifold admits a smooth partition of unity subordinate to it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-complex-spectral-theorem-for-normal-endomorphisms",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional complex inner product space and let $T:V\\to V$\nbe linear. Then $T$ is normal if and only if $V$ has an orthonormal basis\nconsisting of eigenvectors of $T$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-cauchy-integral-formula-circle",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $a\\in\\mathbb C$, let $R>0$, and let $f$ be holomorphic on the disc\n\n$$D(a,R)=\\{\\zeta\\in\\mathbb C:|\\zeta-a|<R\\}.$$\n\nIf $0<r<R$, $|z-a|<r$, and $\\gamma(t)=a+r\\exp(it)$ for $0\\le t\\le2\\pi$, then\n\n$$f(z)=\\frac{1}{2\\pi i}\\int_\\gamma\\frac{f(\\zeta)}{\\zeta-z}\\,d\\zeta.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-first-chern-form-agrees-with-the-topological-line-class",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $M$ be a finite-dimensional Hausdorff\nsecond-countable smooth manifold, possibly with boundary or empty, and let\n$L\\to M$ be a smooth complex line bundle with a supplied Hermitian metric and\nHermitian connection $\\nabla$. Write $\\Omega_\\nabla$ for its curvature and\n$c_1(L):=e(L_{\\mathbb R})$ for the published integral line class, using the\ncomplex orientation on the underlying real plane. Let\n$$\\rho_M:H^2(M;\\mathbb Z)\\longrightarrow H^2(M;\\mathbb R)$$\nbe induced by $\\mathbb Z\\hookrightarrow\\mathbb R$. Under the natural de Rham\nisomorphism $J_M$,\n$$J_M\\left[ -\\frac{\\Omega_\\nabla}{2\\pi i}\\right]=\\rho_M(c_1(L)).$$\nFor a disconnected base the same line-class convention is understood on each\ncomponent; the global class is the Euler class displayed above.\n\nIf $\\nabla'$ is any complex connection on $L$, not necessarily metric\ncompatible, then\n$$\\left[-\\frac{\\Omega_{\\nabla'}}{2\\pi i}\\right]=\\iota_M\\!\\left(J_M^{-1}\\rho_M(c_1(L))\\right)\\quad\\text{in }H^2_{\\rm dR}(M;\\mathbb C),$$\nwhere $\\iota_M$ is induced by including real-valued forms into complex-valued\nforms. Thus the arbitrary-connection class is identified with the same real\nclass by the degree-one transgression.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-chern-classes-from-the-projective-bundle-relation",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume AC. Let $E\\to B$ be a numerable complex rank-$n$ bundle with $n\\geq1$\nover a path-connected CW complex $B$ (or over one of the CW-type bases of\n[[thm-integral-complex-projective-bundle-theorem]]). By that theorem there are\nunique classes $a_i\\in H^{2i}(B;\\mathbb Z)$, $1\\leq i\\leq n$, such that\n$$x_E^{\\,n}-a_1x_E^{\\,n-1}+a_2x_E^{\\,n-2}-\\cdots+(-1)^na_n=0\\qquad\\text{in }H^{2n}(P(E);\\mathbb Z),$$\nwhere $x_E\\in H^2(P(E);\\mathbb Z)$ is the Euler class of the underlying real\nbundle of the tautological line\n([[def-complex-projective-bundle-and-tautological-complex-line]]). The\n**Chern classes** of $E$ are these coefficients:\n$$c_i(E):=a_i\\in H^{2i}(B;\\mathbb Z)\\qquad(1\\leq i\\leq n),$$\ncompleted by the conventions $c_0(E):=1\\in H^0(B;\\mathbb Z)$ and\n$c_i(E):=0$ for $i>n$, and the **total Chern class** is the finite sum\n$c(E):=\\sum_{i\\geq0}c_i(E)=1+c_1(E)+\\cdots+c_n(E)\\in H^*(B;\\mathbb Z)$.\n\nFor the zero bundle of rank $0$ the conventions give $c(0_B)=1$. The definition\ndepends only on the isomorphism class of $E$, because an isomorphism of\nbundles induces an isomorphism of projective bundles pulling $x$ back to $x$\nand hence preserves the unique relation. For a complex line bundle $L\\to B$ one\nhas $P(L)\\cong B$ over $B$ with $\\gamma_L$ corresponding to $L$ under that\nidentification, so the relation is $x_L-c_1(L)=0$; since $x_L=e(L_{\\mathbb R})$\nunder the identification, this gives\n$$c_1(L)=e(L_{\\mathbb R}).$$\nThis is the normalization used throughout the page: the first Chern class of a\ncomplex line is the Euler class of its underlying real bundle in the complex\norientation, not a separately chosen normalization.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-fundamental-class-of-a-compact-oriented-manifold",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $M$ be a compact boundaryless $n$-manifold with a specified $R$-orientation $\\mu$, where $R$ is a commutative unital ring. Its **fundamental class** is\n$$[M]=[M]_M\\in H_n(M,M\\setminus M;R)=H_n(M;R),$$\nusing [[lem-compatible-local-orientation-classes-exist-over-compact-subsets]] with the compact support $K=M$. The final equality holds at the chain-complex level: $C_*(\\varnothing;R)=0$, so quotienting by it leaves $C_*(M;R)$. The cited lemma proves existence and uniqueness of this class with point restriction $\\mu_x$ at every $x\\in M$. Thus the notation denotes a class determined by the supplied orientation, not an arbitrary generator chosen afterward.\n\nThe same lemma gives finitely many open-and-closed components $M_1,\\ldots,M_k$ and the restriction isomorphism\n$$H_n(M;R)\\longrightarrow\\bigoplus_{j=1}^k H_n(M,M\\setminus M_j;R).$$\nThe summand on the right identifies with $H_n(M_j;R)$: apply [[thm-excision-for-singular-homology]] to remove the closed set $M\\setminus M_j$, which is the entire open relative subspace. The resulting pair is $(M_j,\\varnothing)$. Under this identification $[M]$ corresponds to the finite tuple of the component fundamental classes.\n\nIf the orientation is negated on one component, its new fundamental-class summand is the negative of its old one. Indeed restriction is linear, so the negative class restricts to $-\\mu_x$ at all points of that component, and uniqueness in the compact-set lemma identifies it with the new class. The other component summands are unchanged. In characteristic two this negation can leave the orientation and class unchanged; no distinction between $\\mu$ and $-\\mu$ is imposed when they are equal.\n\nFor empty $M$ the fundamental class is $0$ in the zero homology group, with an empty direct sum. For $R=0$ the unique orientation gives the zero class. A compact zero-manifold has finitely many singleton components, and its fundamental zero-cycle has at each point the coefficient prescribed by its local module generator. The definition requires a supplied orientation and uses no AC or simultaneous choice of one orientation per component.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-kronecker-evaluation-pairing",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix a space $X$, abelian group $G$ and integer $n\\ge0$. For a singular cocycle $\\varphi\\in Z^n(X;G)$ and an integral singular cycle $c\\in Z_n(X;\\mathbb Z)$, define their **evaluation** to be $\\varphi(c)\\in G$, using [[def-singular-cohomology-with-coefficients]] and [[def-singular-chain-complex-and-singular-homology]]. If $c=\\sum_\\sigma a_\\sigma\\sigma$ is its finite formal expansion, this value is $\\sum_\\sigma a_\\sigma\\varphi(\\sigma)$.\n\nThe **Kronecker pairing** on quotient classes is the rule\n$$\\langle[\\varphi],[c]\\rangle=\\varphi(c),\\qquad H^n(X;G)\\times H_n(X;\\mathbb Z)\\to G.$$\nIts descent through both quotients, biadditivity and naturality are justified by the following lemma [[lem-the-kronecker-pairing-is-independent-of-cocycle-and-cycle-representatives]], before any use of the quotient pairing. It is not an assertion that evaluation gives an isomorphism to the full dual of homology.\n\nFor negative $n$, the two groups are zero and the pairing is the zero map. Empty $X$ and zero $G$ also give zero evaluation. When $G$ is an $R$-module, the cohomology variable is $R$-linear and the integral homology variable is additive; an $R$-bilinear pairing instead uses chains and homology over $R$ with $R$-linear cochains. No representative-selection function is included in the definition.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-de-rham-theorem",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. For every finite-dimensional Hausdorff second-countable smooth manifold $M$, possibly with boundary, integration on smooth simplices followed by the inverse of restriction from continuous to smooth singular cohomology gives a natural isomorphism of unital graded real algebras\n$$J_M:H^*_{\\mathrm{dR}}(M)\\xrightarrow{\\cong}H^*_{\\mathrm{sing}}(M;\\mathbb R).$$\nThe source product is wedge of form classes, the target product is the front/back singular cup product, and both stars denote direct sums of the homogeneous groups. Naturality is for smooth maps. No compactness assumption is imposed.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "def-de-rham-integration-cochain-map",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For a smooth manifold $M$, possibly with boundary, and $k\\ge0$, define\n$$I_M^k:\\Omega^k(M)\\longrightarrow C_\\infty^k(M;\\mathbb R),\\qquad I_M^k(\\omega)(\\sigma)=\\int_\\sigma\\omega.$$\nOn $c=\\sum_\\sigma a_\\sigma\\sigma$ this means $I_M^k(\\omega)(c)=\\sum_\\sigma a_\\sigma\\int_\\sigma\\omega$, a finite sum. In negative degrees $I_M^k$ is the zero map. Forms and the de Rham complex at a boundary use [[lem-the-de-rham-complex-and-pullback-extend-to-manifolds-with-boundary]]. No orientation of $M$ is required: the domain simplex has the specified orientation. The name integration cochain map is justified by [[thm-de-rham-integration-is-a-cochain-map]].",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "supplier_excerpts": [
