          "status": "Published mathematical supplier; exact current Definition inspected.",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "A **smooth $n$-manifold** is a pair $(M,\\mathcal S)$ in which $M$ is a\ntopological $n$-manifold\n([[def-topological-manifold-without-boundary]]) and $\\mathcal S$ is a **smooth\nstructure** on $M$: a maximal smooth atlas\n([[thm-each-smooth-atlas-is-contained-in-a-unique-maximal-smooth-atlas]]).\nBecause\n[[thm-each-smooth-atlas-is-contained-in-a-unique-maximal-smooth-atlas]] sends\nevery smooth atlas to the unique maximal atlas containing it, a smooth manifold\nis equivalently specified by a topological manifold $M$ together with any one\nsmooth atlas $\\mathcal A$, the structure being the generated $[\\mathcal A]$. A\nchart $(U,\\varphi)\\in\\mathcal S$ is called a **smooth chart** (or a **chart of\nthe smooth structure**); its domain is a **coordinate domain** and its\ncoordinate functions are **smooth coordinates** on $U$. When the structure is\nclear from context, the manifold itself is written $M$ in place of\n$(M,\\mathcal S)$.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[F1] [F1] [[def-smooth-manifold]] and [[def-c-r-and-smooth-maps-between-smooth-manifolds]]: $\\Sigma$ has a finite-dimensional smooth atlas, and $C^2$ means that coordinate representatives have continuous derivatives through order two.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Definition item; no proof is claimed or required for the defining interface.",
          "consumer_use": "[F1] [F1] [[def-smooth-manifold]] and [[def-c-r-and-smooth-maps-between-smooth-manifolds]]: $\\Sigma$ has a finite-dimensional smooth atlas, and $C^2$ means that coordinate representatives have continuous derivatives through order two."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-c-r-and-smooth-maps-between-smooth-manifolds",
          "carrier": "items/def-c-r-and-smooth-maps-between-smooth-manifolds.md",
          "status": "Published mathematical supplier; exact current Definition inspected.",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Let $M$ and $N$ be smooth manifolds, let $F:M\\to N$ be a map, let $p\\in M$ and\nlet $r\\in\\mathbb N_0\\cup\\{\\infty\\}$, where $\\mathbb N_0=\\{0,1,2,\\ldots\\}$.\nFor $m,q\\in\\mathbb N_0$ and an open set $E\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$, a map\n$g:E\\to\\mathbb R^q$ is **of class $C^r$** as follows. When $m=0$, every such\nmap is declared $C^r$. When $m\\ge1$, each component $g_l$ with $l<q$ must be\n$C^r$ in the sense of\n[[def-ck-and-multi-index-notation-in-several-variables]]; when $q=0$ this\ncomponent condition is vacuous.\nSuppose $F$ is continuous at $p$. Then $F$ is **of class $C^r$ at $p$** when\nthere are smooth charts $(U,\\varphi)$ of $M$ at $p$ and $(V,\\psi)$ of $N$ at\n$F(p)$ with $F(U)\\subseteq V$ such that the coordinate representative\n\n$$\\psi\\circ F\\circ\\varphi^{-1}:\\varphi(U)\\longrightarrow\\psi(V)$$\n\n([[def-coordinate-representation-of-a-map]]) is of class $C^r$ on a\nneighbourhood of $\\varphi(p)$ in this componentwise Euclidean sense. By\n[[lem-chart-independence-of-c-r-smoothness]], this condition is independent of\nthe chosen charts: if one such representative is $C^r$, then every one is, so\n\"some charts\" may be read as \"any charts\". A map is **$C^r$ on an open set**\n$W\\subseteq M$ when it is continuous on $W$ and $C^r$ at every point of $W$. A\nmap that is $C^r$ for every finite $r$ — equivalently $C^\\infty$ — is called\n**smooth**; the term **$C^r$ map between smooth manifolds** is reserved for the\ncase where $F$ is continuous and the representative condition holds at every\npoint of $M$.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[F1] [F1] [[def-smooth-manifold]] and [[def-c-r-and-smooth-maps-between-smooth-manifolds]]: $\\Sigma$ has a finite-dimensional smooth atlas, and $C^2$ means that coordinate representatives have continuous derivatives through order two.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Definition item; no proof is claimed or required for the defining interface.",
          "consumer_use": "[F1] [F1] [[def-smooth-manifold]] and [[def-c-r-and-smooth-maps-between-smooth-manifolds]]: $\\Sigma$ has a finite-dimensional smooth atlas, and $C^2$ means that coordinate representatives have continuous derivatives through order two."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-nrqmx-smooth-projector-bundle",
          "carrier": "items/def-nrqmx-smooth-projector-bundle.md",
          "status": "Current Step 1 draft in the assigned batch; exact current carrier inspected.",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Let $M$ be a finite-dimensional smooth manifold, let $d\\geq1$ be an integer, and give $\\mathbb C^d$ its standard Hermitian inner product. Let $P:M\\to\\operatorname{End}(\\mathbb C^d)$ be a $C^2$ map such that $P(x)^2=P(x)=P(x)^*$ and $\\operatorname{rank}P(x)=r$ is constant. Define\n$$E_P=\\{(x,v)\\in M\\times\\mathbb C^d:P(x)v=v\\},\\qquad \\pi(x,v)=x.$$\nIts fiber at $x$ is $\\operatorname{ran}P(x)$ with the inner product inherited from $\\mathbb C^d$. For a $C^1$ section $s:U\\to\\mathbb C^d$ over an open $U\\subseteq M$ satisfying $P s=s$, define the projected connection by $\\nabla s=P\\,ds$, where $d$ is the componentwise exterior derivative. For a $C^2$ path $\\gamma:[0,1]\\to M$, put $P_\\gamma(t)=P(\\gamma(t))$ and $K_\\gamma(t)=[P_\\gamma'(t),P_\\gamma(t)]$. Kato transport along $\\gamma$ is the matrix solution $W_\\gamma'(t)=K_\\gamma(t)W_\\gamma(t)$, $W_\\gamma(0)=I_d$.\n\nThe connection and Kato transport are defined from the projector family itself; no Schrödinger Hamiltonian or physical time evolution is needed. A coherent uniformly gapped spectral projection of a $C^2$ finite Hermitian family is one source of such a $C^2$ family: under Countable Choice, an earlier finite-dimensional adiabatic result supplies that regularity.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[F2] [F2] [[def-nrqmx-smooth-projector-bundle]] and [[thm-nrqmx-berry-projector-curvature]]: $E_P$ is the range bundle, $\\nabla=P\\,d$, and its curvature is $F=P(dP\\wedge dP)P$.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Current Step 1 definition; exact interface and current justified_by argument inspected; independent acceptance is not inferred.",
          "consumer_use": "[F2] [F2] [[def-nrqmx-smooth-projector-bundle]] and [[thm-nrqmx-berry-projector-curvature]]: $E_P$ is the range bundle, $\\nabla=P\\,d$, and its curvature is $F=P(dP\\wedge dP)P$."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-nrqmx-berry-projector-curvature",
          "carrier": "items/thm-nrqmx-berry-projector-curvature.md",
          "status": "Current Step 1 draft in the assigned batch; exact current carrier inspected.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $M$, $d$, and $P$ satisfy [[def-nrqmx-smooth-projector-bundle]], with constant rank $r$. The curvature of the projected connection $\\nabla=P\\,d$, as an endomorphism-valued two-form on $\\mathbb C^d$, is\n$$F=P(dP\\wedge dP)P.$$\nFor any local $C^2$ orthonormal frame $V:U\\to\\mathbb C^{d\\times r}$ with $V^*V=I_r$ and $P=VV^*$, its connection matrix $A=V^*dV$ is anti-Hermitian and its curvature matrix is $F_{\\mathrm{fr}}=dA+A\\wedge A=V^*FV$. If $V'=Vg$ for a $C^2$ map $g:U\\to U(r)$, then $A'=g^{-1}Ag+g^{-1}dg$ and $F'_{\\mathrm{fr}}=g^{-1}F_{\\mathrm{fr}}g$. Along every $C^2$ path, Kato transport is unitary and intertwines the endpoint projectors.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[F2] [F2] [[def-nrqmx-smooth-projector-bundle]] and [[thm-nrqmx-berry-projector-curvature]]: $E_P$ is the range bundle, $\\nabla=P\\,d$, and its curvature is $F=P(dP\\wedge dP)P$.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Current Step 1 draft; complete current proof inspected; independent acceptance is not inferred.",
          "consumer_use": "[F2] [F2] [[def-nrqmx-smooth-projector-bundle]] and [[thm-nrqmx-berry-projector-curvature]]: $E_P$ is the range bundle, $\\nabla=P\\,d$, and its curvature is $F=P(dP\\wedge dP)P$."
        },
        {
