        {
          "supplier": "thm-complex-spectral-theorem-for-normal-endomorphisms",
          "carrier": "items/thm-complex-spectral-theorem-for-normal-endomorphisms.md",
          "status": "Published mathematical supplier; exact current Statement inspected.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional complex inner product space and let $T:V\\to V$\nbe linear. Then $T$ is normal if and only if $V$ has an orthonormal basis\nconsisting of eigenvectors of $T$.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[F4] [F4] [[thm-complex-spectral-theorem-for-normal-endomorphisms]]: a finite-dimensional complex Hermitian matrix has an orthonormal eigenbasis. [[thm-cauchy-integral-formula-circle]] gives the scalar circle integral of $(z-\\lambda)^{-1}$ as $2\\pi i$ for an interior $\\lambda$ and zero for an exterior $\\lambda$.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published result; complete current Statement and proof inspected for the scoped use.",
          "consumer_use": "[F4] [F4] [[thm-complex-spectral-theorem-for-normal-endomorphisms]]: a finite-dimensional complex Hermitian matrix has an orthonormal eigenbasis. [[thm-cauchy-integral-formula-circle]] gives the scalar circle integral of $(z-\\lambda)^{-1}$ as $2\\pi i$ for an interior $\\lambda$ and zero for an exterior $\\lambda$."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-cauchy-integral-formula-circle",
          "carrier": "items/thm-cauchy-integral-formula-circle.md",
          "status": "Published mathematical supplier; exact current Statement inspected.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $a\\in\\mathbb C$, let $R>0$, and let $f$ be holomorphic on the disc\n\n$$D(a,R)=\\{\\zeta\\in\\mathbb C:|\\zeta-a|<R\\}.$$\n\nIf $0<r<R$, $|z-a|<r$, and $\\gamma(t)=a+r\\exp(it)$ for $0\\le t\\le2\\pi$, then\n\n$$f(z)=\\frac{1}{2\\pi i}\\int_\\gamma\\frac{f(\\zeta)}{\\zeta-z}\\,d\\zeta.$$",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[F4] [F4] [[thm-complex-spectral-theorem-for-normal-endomorphisms]]: a finite-dimensional complex Hermitian matrix has an orthonormal eigenbasis. [[thm-cauchy-integral-formula-circle]] gives the scalar circle integral of $(z-\\lambda)^{-1}$ as $2\\pi i$ for an interior $\\lambda$ and zero for an exterior $\\lambda$.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published result; complete current Statement and proof inspected for the scoped use.",
          "consumer_use": "[F4] [F4] [[thm-complex-spectral-theorem-for-normal-endomorphisms]]: a finite-dimensional complex Hermitian matrix has an orthonormal eigenbasis. [[thm-cauchy-integral-formula-circle]] gives the scalar circle integral of $(z-\\lambda)^{-1}$ as $2\\pi i$ for an interior $\\lambda$ and zero for an exterior $\\lambda$."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "lem-first-chern-form-agrees-with-the-topological-line-class",
          "carrier": "items/lem-first-chern-form-agrees-with-the-topological-line-class.md",
          "status": "Published mathematical supplier; exact current Statement inspected.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $M$ be a finite-dimensional Hausdorff\nsecond-countable smooth manifold, possibly with boundary or empty, and let\n$L\\to M$ be a smooth complex line bundle with a supplied Hermitian metric and\nHermitian connection $\\nabla$. Write $\\Omega_\\nabla$ for its curvature and\n$c_1(L):=e(L_{\\mathbb R})$ for the published integral line class, using the\ncomplex orientation on the underlying real plane. Let\n$$\\rho_M:H^2(M;\\mathbb Z)\\longrightarrow H^2(M;\\mathbb R)$$\nbe induced by $\\mathbb Z\\hookrightarrow\\mathbb R$. Under the natural de Rham\nisomorphism $J_M$,\n$$J_M\\left[ -\\frac{\\Omega_\\nabla}{2\\pi i}\\right]=\\rho_M(c_1(L)).$$\nFor a disconnected base the same line-class convention is understood on each\ncomponent; the global class is the Euler class displayed above.\n\nIf $\\nabla'$ is any complex connection on $L$, not necessarily metric\ncompatible, then\n$$\\left[-\\frac{\\Omega_{\\nabla'}}{2\\pi i}\\right]=\\iota_M\\!\\left(J_M^{-1}\\rho_M(c_1(L))\\right)\\quad\\text{in }H^2_{\\rm dR}(M;\\mathbb C),$$\nwhere $\\iota_M$ is induced by including real-valued forms into complex-valued\nforms. Thus the arbitrary-connection class is identified with the same real\nclass by the degree-one transgression.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[F5] [F5] [[lem-first-chern-form-agrees-with-the-topological-line-class]]: under full AC, for a smooth Hermitian line bundle and Hermitian connection with curvature $\\Omega$, $J_\\Sigma[-\\Omega/(2\\pi i)]=\\rho_\\Sigma(c_1)$.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published result; complete current Statement and proof inspected for the scoped use.",
          "consumer_use": "[F5] [F5] [[lem-first-chern-form-agrees-with-the-topological-line-class]]: under full AC, for a smooth Hermitian line bundle and Hermitian connection with curvature $\\Omega$, $J_\\Sigma[-\\Omega/(2\\pi i)]=\\rho_\\Sigma(c_1)$."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-chern-classes-from-the-projective-bundle-relation",
          "carrier": "items/def-chern-classes-from-the-projective-bundle-relation.md",
          "status": "Published mathematical supplier; exact current Definition inspected.",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Assume AC. Let $E\\to B$ be a numerable complex rank-$n$ bundle with $n\\geq1$\nover a path-connected CW complex $B$ (or over one of the CW-type bases of\n[[thm-integral-complex-projective-bundle-theorem]]). By that theorem there are\nunique classes $a_i\\in H^{2i}(B;\\mathbb Z)$, $1\\leq i\\leq n$, such that\n$$x_E^{\\,n}-a_1x_E^{\\,n-1}+a_2x_E^{\\,n-2}-\\cdots+(-1)^na_n=0\\qquad\\text{in }H^{2n}(P(E);\\mathbb Z),$$\nwhere $x_E\\in H^2(P(E);\\mathbb Z)$ is the Euler class of the underlying real\nbundle of the tautological line\n([[def-complex-projective-bundle-and-tautological-complex-line]]). The\n**Chern classes** of $E$ are these coefficients:\n$$c_i(E):=a_i\\in H^{2i}(B;\\mathbb Z)\\qquad(1\\leq i\\leq n),$$\ncompleted by the conventions $c_0(E):=1\\in H^0(B;\\mathbb Z)$ and\n$c_i(E):=0$ for $i>n$, and the **total Chern class** is the finite sum\n$c(E):=\\sum_{i\\geq0}c_i(E)=1+c_1(E)+\\cdots+c_n(E)\\in H^*(B;\\mathbb Z)$.\n\nFor the zero bundle of rank $0$ the conventions give $c(0_B)=1$. The definition\ndepends only on the isomorphism class of $E$, because an isomorphism of\nbundles induces an isomorphism of projective bundles pulling $x$ back to $x$\nand hence preserves the unique relation. For a complex line bundle $L\\to B$ one\nhas $P(L)\\cong B$ over $B$ with $\\gamma_L$ corresponding to $L$ under that\nidentification, so the relation is $x_L-c_1(L)=0$; since $x_L=e(L_{\\mathbb R})$\nunder the identification, this gives\n$$c_1(L)=e(L_{\\mathbb R}).$$\nThis is the normalization used throughout the page: the first Chern class of a\ncomplex line is the Euler class of its underlying real bundle in the complex\norientation, not a separately chosen normalization.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[F6] [F6] [[def-chern-classes-from-the-projective-bundle-relation]]: $c_1$ is invariant under complex bundle isomorphism and for a line is the integral first Chern class. [[def-fundamental-class-of-a-compact-oriented-manifold]] gives $[\\Sigma]\\in H_2(\\Sigma;\\mathbb Z)$, and [[def-kronecker-evaluation-pairing]] evaluates an integral cohomology class on it in $\\mathbb Z$.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Definition item; no proof is claimed or required for the defining interface.",
          "consumer_use": "[F6] [F6] [[def-chern-classes-from-the-projective-bundle-relation]]: $c_1$ is invariant under complex bundle isomorphism and for a line is the integral first Chern class. [[def-fundamental-class-of-a-compact-oriented-manifold]] gives $[\\Sigma]\\in H_2(\\Sigma;\\mathbb Z)$, and [[def-kronecker-evaluation-pairing]] evaluates an integral cohomology class on it in $\\mathbb Z$."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-fundamental-class-of-a-compact-oriented-manifold",
          "carrier": "items/def-fundamental-class-of-a-compact-oriented-manifold.md",
          "status": "Published mathematical supplier; exact current Definition inspected.",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Let $M$ be a compact boundaryless $n$-manifold with a specified $R$-orientation $\\mu$, where $R$ is a commutative unital ring. Its **fundamental class** is\n$$[M]=[M]_M\\in H_n(M,M\\setminus M;R)=H_n(M;R),$$\nusing [[lem-compatible-local-orientation-classes-exist-over-compact-subsets]] with the compact support $K=M$. The final equality holds at the chain-complex level: $C_*(\\varnothing;R)=0$, so quotienting by it leaves $C_*(M;R)$. The cited lemma proves existence and uniqueness of this class with point restriction $\\mu_x$ at every $x\\in M$. Thus the notation denotes a class determined by the supplied orientation, not an arbitrary generator chosen afterward.\n\nThe same lemma gives finitely many open-and-closed components $M_1,\\ldots,M_k$ and the restriction isomorphism\n$$H_n(M;R)\\longrightarrow\\bigoplus_{j=1}^k H_n(M,M\\setminus M_j;R).$$\nThe summand on the right identifies with $H_n(M_j;R)$: apply [[thm-excision-for-singular-homology]] to remove the closed set $M\\setminus M_j$, which is the entire open relative subspace. The resulting pair is $(M_j,\\varnothing)$. Under this identification $[M]$ corresponds to the finite tuple of the component fundamental classes.\n\nIf the orientation is negated on one component, its new fundamental-class summand is the negative of its old one. Indeed restriction is linear, so the negative class restricts to $-\\mu_x$ at all points of that component, and uniqueness in the compact-set lemma identifies it with the new class. The other component summands are unchanged. In characteristic two this negation can leave the orientation and class unchanged; no distinction between $\\mu$ and $-\\mu$ is imposed when they are equal.\n\nFor empty $M$ the fundamental class is $0$ in the zero homology group, with an empty direct sum. For $R=0$ the unique orientation gives the zero class. A compact zero-manifold has finitely many singleton components, and its fundamental zero-cycle has at each point the coefficient prescribed by its local module generator. The definition requires a supplied orientation and uses no AC or simultaneous choice of one orientation per component.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[F6] [F6] [[def-chern-classes-from-the-projective-bundle-relation]]: $c_1$ is invariant under complex bundle isomorphism and for a line is the integral first Chern class. [[def-fundamental-class-of-a-compact-oriented-manifold]] gives $[\\Sigma]\\in H_2(\\Sigma;\\mathbb Z)$, and [[def-kronecker-evaluation-pairing]] evaluates an integral cohomology class on it in $\\mathbb Z$.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Definition item; no proof is claimed or required for the defining interface.",
          "consumer_use": "[F6] [F6] [[def-chern-classes-from-the-projective-bundle-relation]]: $c_1$ is invariant under complex bundle isomorphism and for a line is the integral first Chern class. [[def-fundamental-class-of-a-compact-oriented-manifold]] gives $[\\Sigma]\\in H_2(\\Sigma;\\mathbb Z)$, and [[def-kronecker-evaluation-pairing]] evaluates an integral cohomology class on it in $\\mathbb Z$."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-kronecker-evaluation-pairing",
          "carrier": "items/def-kronecker-evaluation-pairing.md",
          "status": "Published mathematical supplier; exact current Definition inspected.",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Fix a space $X$, abelian group $G$ and integer $n\\ge0$. For a singular cocycle $\\varphi\\in Z^n(X;G)$ and an integral singular cycle $c\\in Z_n(X;\\mathbb Z)$, define their **evaluation** to be $\\varphi(c)\\in G$, using [[def-singular-cohomology-with-coefficients]] and [[def-singular-chain-complex-and-singular-homology]]. If $c=\\sum_\\sigma a_\\sigma\\sigma$ is its finite formal expansion, this value is $\\sum_\\sigma a_\\sigma\\varphi(\\sigma)$.\n\nThe **Kronecker pairing** on quotient classes is the rule\n$$\\langle[\\varphi],[c]\\rangle=\\varphi(c),\\qquad H^n(X;G)\\times H_n(X;\\mathbb Z)\\to G.$$\nIts descent through both quotients, biadditivity and naturality are justified by the following lemma [[lem-the-kronecker-pairing-is-independent-of-cocycle-and-cycle-representatives]], before any use of the quotient pairing. It is not an assertion that evaluation gives an isomorphism to the full dual of homology.\n\nFor negative $n$, the two groups are zero and the pairing is the zero map. Empty $X$ and zero $G$ also give zero evaluation. When $G$ is an $R$-module, the cohomology variable is $R$-linear and the integral homology variable is additive; an $R$-bilinear pairing instead uses chains and homology over $R$ with $R$-linear cochains. No representative-selection function is included in the definition.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[F6] [F6] [[def-chern-classes-from-the-projective-bundle-relation]]: $c_1$ is invariant under complex bundle isomorphism and for a line is the integral first Chern class. [[def-fundamental-class-of-a-compact-oriented-manifold]] gives $[\\Sigma]\\in H_2(\\Sigma;\\mathbb Z)$, and [[def-kronecker-evaluation-pairing]] evaluates an integral cohomology class on it in $\\mathbb Z$.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Definition item; no proof is claimed or required for the defining interface.",
          "consumer_use": "[F6] [F6] [[def-chern-classes-from-the-projective-bundle-relation]]: $c_1$ is invariant under complex bundle isomorphism and for a line is the integral first Chern class. [[def-fundamental-class-of-a-compact-oriented-manifold]] gives $[\\Sigma]\\in H_2(\\Sigma;\\mathbb Z)$, and [[def-kronecker-evaluation-pairing]] evaluates an integral cohomology class on it in $\\mathbb Z$."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-de-rham-theorem",
          "carrier": "items/thm-de-rham-theorem.md",
          "status": "Published mathematical supplier; exact current Statement inspected.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. For every finite-dimensional Hausdorff second-countable smooth manifold $M$, possibly with boundary, integration on smooth simplices followed by the inverse of restriction from continuous to smooth singular cohomology gives a natural isomorphism of unital graded real algebras\n$$J_M:H^*_{\\mathrm{dR}}(M)\\xrightarrow{\\cong}H^*_{\\mathrm{sing}}(M;\\mathbb R).$$\nThe source product is wedge of form classes, the target product is the front/back singular cup product, and both stars denote direct sums of the homogeneous groups. Naturality is for smooth maps. No compactness assumption is imposed.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[F7] [F7] [[thm-de-rham-theorem]] and [[def-de-rham-integration-cochain-map]]: under Countable Choice, the de Rham isomorphism is induced by integration on smooth simplices, so evaluation of $J_\\Sigma[\\alpha]$ on the real fundamental class equals $\\int_\\Sigma\\alpha$.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published result; complete current Statement and proof inspected for the scoped use.",
          "consumer_use": "[F7] [F7] [[thm-de-rham-theorem]] and [[def-de-rham-integration-cochain-map]]: under Countable Choice, the de Rham isomorphism is induced by integration on smooth simplices, so evaluation of $J_\\Sigma[\\alpha]$ on the real fundamental class equals $\\int_\\Sigma\\alpha$."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-de-rham-integration-cochain-map",
          "carrier": "items/def-de-rham-integration-cochain-map.md",
          "status": "Published mathematical supplier; exact current Definition inspected.",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "For a smooth manifold $M$, possibly with boundary, and $k\\ge0$, define\n$$I_M^k:\\Omega^k(M)\\longrightarrow C_\\infty^k(M;\\mathbb R),\\qquad I_M^k(\\omega)(\\sigma)=\\int_\\sigma\\omega.$$\nOn $c=\\sum_\\sigma a_\\sigma\\sigma$ this means $I_M^k(\\omega)(c)=\\sum_\\sigma a_\\sigma\\int_\\sigma\\omega$, a finite sum. In negative degrees $I_M^k$ is the zero map. Forms and the de Rham complex at a boundary use [[lem-the-de-rham-complex-and-pullback-extend-to-manifolds-with-boundary]]. No orientation of $M$ is required: the domain simplex has the specified orientation. The name integration cochain map is justified by [[thm-de-rham-integration-is-a-cochain-map]].",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[F7] [F7] [[thm-de-rham-theorem]] and [[def-de-rham-integration-cochain-map]]: under Countable Choice, the de Rham isomorphism is induced by integration on smooth simplices, so evaluation of $J_\\Sigma[\\alpha]$ on the real fundamental class equals $\\int_\\Sigma\\alpha$.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Definition item; no proof is claimed or required for the defining interface.",
          "consumer_use": "[F7] [F7] [[thm-de-rham-theorem]] and [[def-de-rham-integration-cochain-map]]: under Countable Choice, the de Rham isomorphism is induced by integration on smooth simplices, so evaluation of $J_\\Sigma[\\alpha]$ on the real fundamental class equals $\\int_\\Sigma\\alpha$."
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Choose a finite coordinate-chart cover of the compact surface $\\Sigma$ and a smooth partition $\\{\\phi_j\\}_{j=1}^N$ subordinate to it by [F3]. In each chart, every matrix entry of $\\phi_jP$ is a $C^2$ function supported compactly inside the coordinate domain; extend it by zero to $\\mathbb R^2$. Start with $\\rho(y)=c\\exp(-1/(1-|y|^2))$ for $|y|<1$ and $\\rho(y)=0$ otherwise, where $c>0$ normalizes its integral to one. The extension is smooth because every derivative at the boundary is a finite sum of terms bounded by $t^{-m}e^{-1/t}$ as $t\\downarrow0$, and these tend to zero. Put $\\rho_\\epsilon(y)=\\epsilon^{-2}\\rho(y/\\epsilon)$ and convolve each real and imaginary entry with it. For every multi-index $\\alpha$ with $|\\alpha|\\leq2$, $\\partial^\\alpha(\\rho_\\epsilon*f)=\\rho_\\epsilon*(\\partial^\\alpha f)$ and\n$$\\|\\rho_\\epsilon*(\\partial^\\alpha f)-\\partial^\\alpha f\\|_\\infty\\leq\\sup_{|y|\\leq\\epsilon}\\|\\partial^\\alpha f(\\,\\cdot-y)-\\partial^\\alpha f\\|_\\infty\\longrightarrow0,$$\nbecause each compactly supported derivative is uniformly continuous. For sufficiently small $\\epsilon$, the convolved supports remain inside their charts. Summing the finitely many pulled-back approximants gives a smooth matrix field $Q_\\epsilon\\to P$ in $C^2$; replacing it by $(Q_\\epsilon+Q_\\epsilon^*)/2$ keeps it smooth Hermitian and preserves this convergence.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "construct",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "Fix $\\epsilon$ so $\\delta:=\\sup_\\Sigma\\|Q_\\epsilon-P\\|<1/4$. For each $x\\in\\Sigma$, define\n$$R_\\epsilon(x)=\\frac1{2\\pi i}\\oint_{|z-1|=1/2}(zI-Q_\\epsilon(x))^{-1}\\,dz.$$\nOn this contour $\\|(zI-P(x))^{-1}\\|=2$, so the Neumann series shows $zI-Q_\\epsilon(x)$ is invertible and its inverse has norm at most $4$. The finite-dimensional spectral theorem [F4] diagonalizes $Q_\\epsilon(x)$; the scalar circle integral in [F4] then shows $R_\\epsilon(x)$ is the orthogonal spectral projection onto eigenvalues inside the circle. The same contour is valid for $Q_t=P+t(Q_\\epsilon-P)$ for every $t\\in[0,1]$, so these projections vary continuously with $t$; their integer-valued ranks are constant and equal the rank one of $P$. The resolvent identity gives\n$$\\|R_\\epsilon-P\\|_\\infty\\leq\\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\,\\pi\\,(4)\\delta(2)=4\\delta<1.$$\nThe contour integral also shows $R_\\epsilon$ is smooth in $x$.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F4",
            "construct",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "The restriction $R_\\epsilon(x)|_{\\operatorname{ran}P(x)}$ is injective: if $R_\\epsilon v=0$ and $P v=v$, then $\\|v\\|=\\|(P-R_\\epsilon)v\\|\\leq\\|P-R_\\epsilon\\|\\,\\|v\\|$, which forces $v=0$. Both ranges are one-dimensional, so these restrictions form a continuous complex line-bundle isomorphism $E_P\\cong E_{R_\\epsilon}$ and [F6] gives $c_1(E_P)=c_1(E_{R_\\epsilon})$. In a local unit frame $u$ for $E_{R_\\epsilon}$, its connection form is $A=u^*du$ and its scalar curvature is $dA$. The rank-one curvature matrix is $F_{R_\\epsilon}=u(dA)u^*$, hence $\\operatorname{tr}F_{R_\\epsilon}=dA=\\Omega_{\\nabla_\\epsilon}$. Applying [F5] to the smooth Hermitian line bundle $E_{R_\\epsilon}$ and its projected Hermitian connection, then evaluating on $[\\Sigma]$ using [F6] and [F7], gives\n$$\\frac{i}{2\\pi}\\int_\\Sigma\\operatorname{tr}F_{R_\\epsilon}=\\langle c_1(E_{R_\\epsilon}),[\\Sigma]\\rangle=\\langle c_1(E_P),[\\Sigma]\\rangle\\in\\mathbb Z.$$",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "step 1.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "The contour formula for $R_\\epsilon$ and its first two coordinate derivatives show $R_\\epsilon\\to P$ in $C^2$: differentiating $(zI-Q_\\epsilon)^{-1}$ once gives $(zI-Q_\\epsilon)^{-1}(dQ_\\epsilon)(zI-Q_\\epsilon)^{-1}$, and differentiating again gives a finite sum of products of bounded resolvents with first and second derivatives of $Q_\\epsilon$. Thus $F_{R_\\epsilon}=R_\\epsilon(dR_\\epsilon\\wedge dR_\\epsilon)R_\\epsilon$ converges uniformly to $F_P$. Integration over compact $\\Sigma$ is continuous under uniform convergence, while step 2.1 gives the same integer for every sufficiently small $\\epsilon$. Taking the limit proves the stated equality for $P$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.2",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
