          "status": "Current Step 1 draft in the assigned batch; exact current carrier inspected.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $M$ be a compact oriented smooth surface with boundary, and let $D\\subseteq M$ be a compact oriented region whose boundary consists of finitely many $C^2$ arcs meeting at finitely many corners. Let $\\eta=a\\,dx+b\\,dy$ be a complex-valued $C^1$ one-form on a neighborhood of $D$ in each local chart, where $C^1$ means its coefficients and first partial derivatives are continuous up to the boundary. Define $d\\eta=(\\partial_xb-\\partial_ya)\\,dx\\wedge dy$ in each chart; the chain rule and symmetry of second derivatives make this expression coordinate-independent. Give $\\partial D$ the outward-normal-first orientation. Then\n$$\\int_D d\\eta=\\int_{\\partial D}\\eta.$$",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[F4] [F4] [[lem-nrqmx-c1-stokes-compact-surface]]: Stokes holds for the $C^1$ connection forms on the two compact subsurfaces, including their piecewise-$C^2$ boundaries.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Current Step 1 draft; complete current proof inspected; independent acceptance is not inferred.",
          "consumer_use": "[F4] [F4] [[lem-nrqmx-c1-stokes-compact-surface]]: Stokes holds for the $C^1$ connection forms on the two compact subsurfaces, including their piecewise-$C^2$ boundaries."
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "The first equality and integrality are exactly [F2], with the Chern-form convention and sign fixed by [F3].",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "given"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "Let $\\gamma$ lie in one frame patch with unit frame $u$. A section is $u c$, and $\\nabla(uc)=u(dc+A c)$ for $A=u^*du$; therefore a parallel section satisfies $c'=-A(\\gamma')c$. Since this is a scalar equation, its transport factor on that segment is $\\exp(-\\int_\\gamma A)$. The compact path has a finite subdivision into local frame patches by compactness and the open-cover property; uniqueness on each segment follows from the Kato ODE in [F1].",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "given",
            "construct",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "At a frame change $u_{j+1}=u_jg_j$, equality of the represented section $u_jc_j=u_{j+1}c_{j+1}$ gives $c_{j+1}=g_j^{-1}c_j$. Composing the local scalar transport equations from step 1.2 with these coefficient changes gives exactly the product in the Statement. Refining the subdivision inserts a segment and its inverse transition at the same point, so the product is unchanged; equivalently, it is the unique transport from the Kato ODE in [F1].",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "step 1.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "In the two-subsurface decomposition, set $A_N=u_N^*du_N$ and $A_S=u_S^*du_S$. From $u_S=u_Ng$ and $g^{-1}g=1$, the product rule gives $A_S=A_N+g^{-1}dg$. For a line, $A\\wedge A=0$, so the scalar curvature on each patch is $dA$. The common boundary has opposite induced orientations on the two subsurfaces. Applying [F4] gives\n$\\int_\\Sigma\\operatorname{tr}F=\\int_\\Gamma A_N+\\int_{-\\Gamma}A_S=\\int_\\Gamma(A_N-A_S)=-\\int_\\Gamma g^{-1}dg.$\nFor one oriented component parameterized by $t\\in[0,2\\pi]$, $|g(t)|=1$ implies $g^{-1}g'=ia(t)$ for a real continuous function $a$. Put $\\theta(t)=\\int_0^t a(s)\\,ds$; differentiating shows $g(t)e^{-i\\theta(t)}$ is constant, so $g(t)=g(0)e^{i\\theta(t)}$. Since $g(2\\pi)=g(0)$, $\\theta(2\\pi)\\in2\\pi\\mathbb Z$, and hence $(2\\pi i)^{-1}\\int g^{-1}dg\\in\\mathbb Z$. Summing components shows $\\operatorname{wind}(g)\\in\\mathbb Z$. Multiplying the curvature identity by $i/(2\\pi)$ and using step 1.1 proves the claimed Chern-number formula, with the sign fixed by the orientation of $\\partial\\Sigma_N$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
