    "ex-nrqmx-spin-half-monopole": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> (Choice function).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of Choice function: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-nrqmx-berry-projector-curvature",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $M$, $d$, and $P$ satisfy [[def-nrqmx-smooth-projector-bundle]], with constant rank $r$. The curvature of the projected connection $\\nabla=P\\,d$, as an endomorphism-valued two-form on $\\mathbb C^d$, is\n$$F=P(dP\\wedge dP)P.$$\nFor any local $C^2$ orthonormal frame $V:U\\to\\mathbb C^{d\\times r}$ with $V^*V=I_r$ and $P=VV^*$, its connection matrix $A=V^*dV$ is anti-Hermitian and its curvature matrix is $F_{\\mathrm{fr}}=dA+A\\wedge A=V^*FV$. If $V'=Vg$ for a $C^2$ map $g:U\\to U(r)$, then $A'=g^{-1}Ag+g^{-1}dg$ and $F'_{\\mathrm{fr}}=g^{-1}F_{\\mathrm{fr}}g$. Along every $C^2$ path, Kato transport is unitary and intertwines the endpoint projectors.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-nrqmx-rank-one-chern-holonomy",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume full AC. Let $\\Sigma$ be a compact boundaryless oriented smooth surface, and let $P:\\Sigma\\to\\operatorname{End}(\\mathbb C^d)$ be a $C^2$ rank-one orthogonal projection family. Let $E_P$ be its complex line bundle, $\\nabla=P\\,d$, and $F=P(dP\\wedge dP)P$. Then\n$$\\frac{i}{2\\pi}\\int_\\Sigma\\operatorname{tr}F=\\langle c_1(E_P),[\\Sigma]\\rangle\\in\\mathbb Z.$$\nFor any $C^2$ closed path $\\gamma$ subdivided into local unit frames $u_j$, with $A_j=u_j^*du_j$ and transition $u_{j+1}=u_jg_j$ for $g_j$ valued in $U(1)$ at the crossing points, the holonomy is the scalar by which parallel transport acts on its one-dimensional initial fiber. This scalar is the product of the local factors $\\exp(-\\int_{\\gamma_j}A_j)$ and the transition factors $g_j^{-1}$. Thus geometric holonomy includes the frame-transition scalars whenever one frame does not cover the path.\n\nIn addition, suppose $\\Sigma=\\Sigma_N\\cup\\Sigma_S$ is a decomposition into compact oriented subsurfaces with disjoint interiors and common finite boundary $\\Gamma=\\partial\\Sigma_N=-\\partial\\Sigma_S$, a finite union of oriented smooth circles. If $E_P$ has supplied $C^2$ unit frames $u_N,u_S$ on these subsurfaces and $u_S=u_Ng$ on $\\Gamma$ for a $C^2$ map $g:\\Gamma\\to U(1)$, then\n$$\\langle c_1(E_P),[\\Sigma]\\rangle=\\operatorname{wind}(g):=\\sum_{\\Gamma_j\\subset\\Gamma}\\frac{1}{2\\pi i}\\int_{\\Gamma_j}g^{-1}dg\\in\\mathbb Z,$$\nwhere each component $\\Gamma_j$ has the orientation induced by $\\partial\\Sigma_N$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-nrqmx-c1-stokes-compact-surface",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $M$ be a compact oriented smooth surface with boundary, and let $D\\subseteq M$ be a compact oriented region whose boundary consists of finitely many $C^2$ arcs meeting at finitely many corners. Let $\\eta=a\\,dx+b\\,dy$ be a complex-valued $C^1$ one-form on a neighborhood of $D$ in each local chart, where $C^1$ means its coefficients and first partial derivatives are continuous up to the boundary. Define $d\\eta=(\\partial_xb-\\partial_ya)\\,dx\\wedge dy$ in each chart; the chain rule and symmetry of second derivatives make this expression coordinate-independent. Give $\\partial D$ the outward-normal-first orientation. Then\n$$\\int_D d\\eta=\\int_{\\partial D}\\eta.$$",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-nrqmx-spin-half-solid-angle-period",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume full AC. Let $S^2$ be the unit sphere with outward area form $\\omega$, and set\n$$\\sigma_x=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad\\sigma_y=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\\\i&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad\\sigma_z=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&-1\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad P_+(n)=\\frac{I+n_x\\sigma_x+n_y\\sigma_y+n_z\\sigma_z}{2}.$$\nFor any two $C^2$ maps $f_0,f_1:D^2\\to S^2$ from an oriented disk, with the same boundary map and its induced boundary orientation, the two oriented solid-angle integrals differ by a multiple of $4\\pi$:\n$$\\int_{D^2}f_0^*\\omega-\\int_{D^2}f_1^*\\omega\\in4\\pi\\mathbb Z.$$",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-heine-borel-rn",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$ with $n \\ge 1$, let $\\mathbb{R}^n$ be the set of functions\n$n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ and let $d_2$ be the Euclidean metric on it\n($\\mathbb{R}^n$ as the set of functions $n \\to \\mathbb{R}$, and $d_1$, $d_2$, $d_\\infty$ are metrics on it). Then:\n\n1. **Closed boxes are compact.** For reals $a_k \\le b_k$ $(k < n)$ the box\n   $Q = \\{\\, x \\in \\mathbb{R}^n : a_k \\le x_k \\le b_k \\text{ for every } k < n \\,\\}$\n   is a compact subset of $(\\mathbb{R}^n, d_2)$ (Open cover, subcover, compact metric space, and compact subset of a metric space).\n2. **Heine-Borel.** A subset $K \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^n$ is a compact subset of\n   $(\\mathbb{R}^n, d_2)$ if and only if $K$ is closed in $\\mathbb{R}^n$\n   (The metric topology: a set is open when every one of its points has a ball around it inside the set; closed means open complement) and bounded\n   (Bounded subset, diameter, distance from a point to a set, and distance between two sets in a metric space).\n3. **The real line.** A subset $K \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ is a compact subset of\n   $(\\mathbb{R}, d_{\\mathbb{R}})$, the usual metric $d_{\\mathbb{R}}(x,y) = |x-y|$\n   (The absolute value makes $\\mathbb{R}$ a metric space: $d(x,y) = |x-y|$ is a metric, its open balls are the intervals $(x-r, x+r)$, and it is unbounded), if and only if $K$ is closed in\n   $\\mathbb{R}$ and bounded.\n\n**No choice principle is used.** The bisection below halves one coordinate at a\ntime and takes the **left** half whenever the left half still fails to be finitely\ncovered, the right half otherwise: a rule with two outcomes, decided by a\nproperty of the box, not a selection. That is the whole reason the theorem is\navailable in ZF, while the general \"complete and totally bounded implies compact\"\n(A complete, totally bounded metric space is compact, proved from countable choice used exactly once) is not.\n\nThe hypothesis $n \\ge 1$ is inherited from $\\mathbb{R}^n$ as the set of functions $n \\to \\mathbb{R}$, and $d_1$, $d_2$, $d_\\infty$ are metrics on it, which defines\n$\\mathbb{R}^n$ and its metrics only there; the last remark below records what\nhappens at $n = 0$.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-lebesgue-number-lemma",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,d)$ be a compact metric space ([[def-metric-compactness]],\n[[def-metric-space]]) and let $\\mathcal{U}$ be an open cover of $X$. Then there\nis a real $\\delta > 0$, a **Lebesgue number** for $\\mathcal{U}$, such that every\nnonempty $A \\subseteq X$ with $\\operatorname{diam}(A) < \\delta$\n([[def-metric-bounded-diameter]]) satisfies $A \\subseteq U$ for some\n$U \\in \\mathcal{U}$.\n\nDiameters of nonempty subsets of $X$ are defined because a compact space is\nbounded ([[thm-compact-subset-is-closed-and-bounded]]) and a subset of a bounded\nset is bounded. No choice principle is used.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "supplier_excerpts": [
        {
          "supplier": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "carrier": "items/def-axiom-of-choice.md",
          "status": "Published mathematical supplier; exact current Definition inspected.",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> (Choice function).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[A1] [A1] Full AC is [[def-axiom-of-choice]]; it entails Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]].",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Definition item; no proof is claimed or required for the defining interface.",
          "consumer_use": "[A1] [A1] Full AC is [[def-axiom-of-choice]]; it entails Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]]."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-countable-choice",
          "carrier": "items/def-countable-choice.md",
          "status": "Published mathematical supplier; exact current Definition inspected.",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of Choice function: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets has a choice function.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[A1] [A1] Full AC is [[def-axiom-of-choice]]; it entails Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]].",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Definition item; no proof is claimed or required for the defining interface.",
          "consumer_use": "[A1] [A1] Full AC is [[def-axiom-of-choice]]; it entails Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]]."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-nrqmx-berry-projector-curvature",
          "carrier": "items/thm-nrqmx-berry-projector-curvature.md",
          "status": "Current Step 1 draft in the assigned batch; exact current carrier inspected.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $M$, $d$, and $P$ satisfy [[def-nrqmx-smooth-projector-bundle]], with constant rank $r$. The curvature of the projected connection $\\nabla=P\\,d$, as an endomorphism-valued two-form on $\\mathbb C^d$, is\n$$F=P(dP\\wedge dP)P.$$\nFor any local $C^2$ orthonormal frame $V:U\\to\\mathbb C^{d\\times r}$ with $V^*V=I_r$ and $P=VV^*$, its connection matrix $A=V^*dV$ is anti-Hermitian and its curvature matrix is $F_{\\mathrm{fr}}=dA+A\\wedge A=V^*FV$. If $V'=Vg$ for a $C^2$ map $g:U\\to U(r)$, then $A'=g^{-1}Ag+g^{-1}dg$ and $F'_{\\mathrm{fr}}=g^{-1}F_{\\mathrm{fr}}g$. Along every $C^2$ path, Kato transport is unitary and intertwines the endpoint projectors.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[F1] [F1] [[thm-nrqmx-berry-projector-curvature]]: for a $C^2$ orthogonal projector, the projected connection has curvature $P(dP\\wedge dP)P$ and a local orthonormal frame $u$ has connection form $u^*du$.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Current Step 1 draft; complete current proof inspected; independent acceptance is not inferred.",
          "consumer_use": "[F1] [F1] [[thm-nrqmx-berry-projector-curvature]]: for a $C^2$ orthogonal projector, the projected connection has curvature $P(dP\\wedge dP)P$ and a local orthonormal frame $u$ has connection form $u^*du$."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-nrqmx-rank-one-chern-holonomy",
          "carrier": "items/thm-nrqmx-rank-one-chern-holonomy.md",
          "status": "Current Step 1 draft in the assigned batch; exact current carrier inspected.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Assume full AC. Let $\\Sigma$ be a compact boundaryless oriented smooth surface, and let $P:\\Sigma\\to\\operatorname{End}(\\mathbb C^d)$ be a $C^2$ rank-one orthogonal projection family. Let $E_P$ be its complex line bundle, $\\nabla=P\\,d$, and $F=P(dP\\wedge dP)P$. Then\n$$\\frac{i}{2\\pi}\\int_\\Sigma\\operatorname{tr}F=\\langle c_1(E_P),[\\Sigma]\\rangle\\in\\mathbb Z.$$\nFor any $C^2$ closed path $\\gamma$ subdivided into local unit frames $u_j$, with $A_j=u_j^*du_j$ and transition $u_{j+1}=u_jg_j$ for $g_j$ valued in $U(1)$ at the crossing points, the holonomy is the scalar by which parallel transport acts on its one-dimensional initial fiber. This scalar is the product of the local factors $\\exp(-\\int_{\\gamma_j}A_j)$ and the transition factors $g_j^{-1}$. Thus geometric holonomy includes the frame-transition scalars whenever one frame does not cover the path.\n\nIn addition, suppose $\\Sigma=\\Sigma_N\\cup\\Sigma_S$ is a decomposition into compact oriented subsurfaces with disjoint interiors and common finite boundary $\\Gamma=\\partial\\Sigma_N=-\\partial\\Sigma_S$, a finite union of oriented smooth circles. If $E_P$ has supplied $C^2$ unit frames $u_N,u_S$ on these subsurfaces and $u_S=u_Ng$ on $\\Gamma$ for a $C^2$ map $g:\\Gamma\\to U(1)$, then\n$$\\langle c_1(E_P),[\\Sigma]\\rangle=\\operatorname{wind}(g):=\\sum_{\\Gamma_j\\subset\\Gamma}\\frac{1}{2\\pi i}\\int_{\\Gamma_j}g^{-1}dg\\in\\mathbb Z,$$\nwhere each component $\\Gamma_j$ has the orientation induced by $\\partial\\Sigma_N$.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[F2] [F2] [[thm-nrqmx-rank-one-chern-holonomy]]: the rank-one Chern number is the curvature integral with factor $i/(2\\pi)$, and local parallel transport has factors $\\exp(-\\int A)$ with inverse frame-transition scalars at patch changes.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Current Step 1 draft; complete current proof inspected; independent acceptance is not inferred.",
          "consumer_use": "[F2] [F2] [[thm-nrqmx-rank-one-chern-holonomy]]: the rank-one Chern number is the curvature integral with factor $i/(2\\pi)$, and local parallel transport has factors $\\exp(-\\int A)$ with inverse frame-transition scalars at patch changes."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "lem-nrqmx-c1-stokes-compact-surface",
          "carrier": "items/lem-nrqmx-c1-stokes-compact-surface.md",
          "status": "Current Step 1 draft in the assigned batch; exact current carrier inspected.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $M$ be a compact oriented smooth surface with boundary, and let $D\\subseteq M$ be a compact oriented region whose boundary consists of finitely many $C^2$ arcs meeting at finitely many corners. Let $\\eta=a\\,dx+b\\,dy$ be a complex-valued $C^1$ one-form on a neighborhood of $D$ in each local chart, where $C^1$ means its coefficients and first partial derivatives are continuous up to the boundary. Define $d\\eta=(\\partial_xb-\\partial_ya)\\,dx\\wedge dy$ in each chart; the chain rule and symmetry of second derivatives make this expression coordinate-independent. Give $\\partial D$ the outward-normal-first orientation. Then\n$$\\int_D d\\eta=\\int_{\\partial D}\\eta.$$",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[F3] [F3] [[lem-nrqmx-c1-stokes-compact-surface]]: for a $C^1$ one-form on a compact oriented surface region with finitely many piecewise-$C^2$ boundary arcs and corners, Stokes holds with the outward-normal-first orientation.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Current Step 1 draft; complete current proof inspected; independent acceptance is not inferred.",
          "consumer_use": "[F3] [F3] [[lem-nrqmx-c1-stokes-compact-surface]]: for a $C^1$ one-form on a compact oriented surface region with finitely many piecewise-$C^2$ boundary arcs and corners, Stokes holds with the outward-normal-first orientation."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "lem-nrqmx-spin-half-solid-angle-period",
          "carrier": "items/lem-nrqmx-spin-half-solid-angle-period.md",
          "status": "Current Step 1 draft in the assigned batch; exact current carrier inspected.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Assume full AC. Let $S^2$ be the unit sphere with outward area form $\\omega$, and set\n$$\\sigma_x=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad\\sigma_y=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\\\i&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad\\sigma_z=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&-1\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad P_+(n)=\\frac{I+n_x\\sigma_x+n_y\\sigma_y+n_z\\sigma_z}{2}.$$\nFor any two $C^2$ maps $f_0,f_1:D^2\\to S^2$ from an oriented disk, with the same boundary map and its induced boundary orientation, the two oriented solid-angle integrals differ by a multiple of $4\\pi$:\n$$\\int_{D^2}f_0^*\\omega-\\int_{D^2}f_1^*\\omega\\in4\\pi\\mathbb Z.$$",
