      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Matrix multiplication gives $(n\\cdot\\sigma)^*=(n\\cdot\\sigma)$ and $(n\\cdot\\sigma)^2=I$, while $\\operatorname{tr}(n\\cdot\\sigma)=0$. Hence $P_+^*=P_+^2=P_+$ and $\\operatorname{tr}P_+=1$, so it is a rank-one orthogonal projection. Direct multiplication gives $(n\\cdot\\sigma)u_N=u_N$ and $u_N^*u_N=1$, so $u_N$ is a normalized local frame of its positive eigenline.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Differentiating the displayed spinor gives $u_N^*du_N=i\\sin^2(\\theta/2)\\,d\\phi=i(1-\\cos\\theta)d\\phi/2$. Taking the exterior derivative yields $dA_N=i\\sin\\theta\\,d\\theta\\wedge d\\phi/2$, which is the curvature in this frame by [F1]. Both the projector curvature and the spherical area form are global smooth two-forms, so their equality on the northern chart gives $F=(i/2)\\omega_{S^2}$ globally.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Integrating the area form gives $\\int_{S^2}\\sin\\theta\\,d\\theta\\,d\\phi=4\\pi$, hence $\\frac{i}{2\\pi}\\int_{S^2}F=\\frac{i}{2\\pi}\\frac{i}{2}(4\\pi)=-1$. By [F2] this is the integral first Chern number with the stated anti-Hermitian connection convention.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "A southern frame is $u_S=(e^{-i\\phi}\\cos(\\theta/2),\\sin(\\theta/2))^T=e^{-i\\phi}u_N$; it is regular at the south pole and together with $u_N$ covers $S^2$. Along the equator the transition is $g(\\phi)=e^{-i\\phi}$, so $(2\\pi i)^{-1}\\int_0^{2\\pi}g^{-1}dg=-1$. The two-hemisphere clutching clause in [F2] therefore gives Chern number $-1$, matching the direct curvature integral in step 3.1 and fixing the orientation sign. Pull these frames back to a chosen oriented disk filling $S$. By [F5], the inverse images cover the compact parameter disk and have a positive Lebesgue number; barycentric subdivision of a finite triangulation has mesh tending to zero (each subdivision reduces every triangle diameter by at least the factor $2/3$). Thus each closed cell eventually lies in one frame patch. On a cell $\\Delta$, its frame connection $A_\\Delta$ is $C^1$ because the filling is $C^2$. By [F3], $\\int_{\\partial\\Delta}A_\\Delta=\\int_\\Delta F$. Since one frame covers the cell, its boundary holonomy is $\\exp(-\\int_{\\partial\\Delta}A_\\Delta)=\\exp(-\\int_\\Delta F)$. Choose a unit vector at each triangulation vertex and write each oriented edge transport as its unit-modulus scalar in those endpoint vectors. In the product of the cell-boundary holonomies every internal edge occurs once in each direction, so its scalar and inverse cancel; only the outer boundary loop remains. Thus $\\operatorname{Hol}(C)=\\exp(-\\int_SF)=\\exp(-i\\Omega/2)$. If a second filling is chosen, [F4] says its solid angle differs by $4\\pi k$ for some integer $k$, so this exponential is independent of the filling. For a latitude circle the north-cap integral is $\\Omega=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_0^\\theta\\sin u\\,du\\,d\\phi=2\\pi(1-\\cos\\theta)$.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
