          "exact_statement": "Let $M$ and $N$ be smooth manifolds, let $F:M\\to N$ be a map, let $p\\in M$ and\nlet $r\\in\\mathbb N_0\\cup\\{\\infty\\}$, where $\\mathbb N_0=\\{0,1,2,\\ldots\\}$.\nFor $m,q\\in\\mathbb N_0$ and an open set $E\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$, a map\n$g:E\\to\\mathbb R^q$ is **of class $C^r$** as follows. When $m=0$, every such\nmap is declared $C^r$. When $m\\ge1$, each component $g_l$ with $l<q$ must be\n$C^r$ in the sense of\n[[def-ck-and-multi-index-notation-in-several-variables]]; when $q=0$ this\ncomponent condition is vacuous.\nSuppose $F$ is continuous at $p$. Then $F$ is **of class $C^r$ at $p$** when\nthere are smooth charts $(U,\\varphi)$ of $M$ at $p$ and $(V,\\psi)$ of $N$ at\n$F(p)$ with $F(U)\\subseteq V$ such that the coordinate representative\n\n$$\\psi\\circ F\\circ\\varphi^{-1}:\\varphi(U)\\longrightarrow\\psi(V)$$\n\n([[def-coordinate-representation-of-a-map]]) is of class $C^r$ on a\nneighbourhood of $\\varphi(p)$ in this componentwise Euclidean sense. By\n[[lem-chart-independence-of-c-r-smoothness]], this condition is independent of\nthe chosen charts: if one such representative is $C^r$, then every one is, so\n\"some charts\" may be read as \"any charts\". A map is **$C^r$ on an open set**\n$W\\subseteq M$ when it is continuous on $W$ and $C^r$ at every point of $W$. A\nmap that is $C^r$ for every finite $r$ — equivalently $C^\\infty$ — is called\n**smooth**; the term **$C^r$ map between smooth manifolds** is reserved for the\ncase where $F$ is continuous and the representative condition holds at every\npoint of $M$.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[F1] [F1] [[def-smooth-chart-atlas-and-structure-on-a-manifold-with-boundary]] and [[def-c-r-and-smooth-maps-between-smooth-manifolds]]: boundary charts take values in a closed half-space, and $C^1$ means that the function and first coordinate partial derivatives extend continuously to its boundary.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Definition item; no proof is claimed or required for the defining interface.",
          "consumer_use": "[F1] [F1] [[def-smooth-chart-atlas-and-structure-on-a-manifold-with-boundary]] and [[def-c-r-and-smooth-maps-between-smooth-manifolds]]: boundary charts take values in a closed half-space, and $C^1$ means that the function and first coordinate partial derivatives extend continuously to its boundary."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-heine-borel-rn",
          "carrier": "items/thm-heine-borel-rn.md",
          "status": "Published mathematical supplier; exact current Statement inspected.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$ with $n \\ge 1$, let $\\mathbb{R}^n$ be the set of functions\n$n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ and let $d_2$ be the Euclidean metric on it\n($\\mathbb{R}^n$ as the set of functions $n \\to \\mathbb{R}$, and $d_1$, $d_2$, $d_\\infty$ are metrics on it). Then:\n\n1. **Closed boxes are compact.** For reals $a_k \\le b_k$ $(k < n)$ the box\n   $Q = \\{\\, x \\in \\mathbb{R}^n : a_k \\le x_k \\le b_k \\text{ for every } k < n \\,\\}$\n   is a compact subset of $(\\mathbb{R}^n, d_2)$ (Open cover, subcover, compact metric space, and compact subset of a metric space).\n2. **Heine-Borel.** A subset $K \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^n$ is a compact subset of\n   $(\\mathbb{R}^n, d_2)$ if and only if $K$ is closed in $\\mathbb{R}^n$\n   (The metric topology: a set is open when every one of its points has a ball around it inside the set; closed means open complement) and bounded\n   (Bounded subset, diameter, distance from a point to a set, and distance between two sets in a metric space).\n3. **The real line.** A subset $K \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ is a compact subset of\n   $(\\mathbb{R}, d_{\\mathbb{R}})$, the usual metric $d_{\\mathbb{R}}(x,y) = |x-y|$\n   (The absolute value makes $\\mathbb{R}$ a metric space: $d(x,y) = |x-y|$ is a metric, its open balls are the intervals $(x-r, x+r)$, and it is unbounded), if and only if $K$ is closed in\n   $\\mathbb{R}$ and bounded.\n\n**No choice principle is used.** The bisection below halves one coordinate at a\ntime and takes the **left** half whenever the left half still fails to be finitely\ncovered, the right half otherwise: a rule with two outcomes, decided by a\nproperty of the box, not a selection. That is the whole reason the theorem is\navailable in ZF, while the general \"complete and totally bounded implies compact\"\n(A complete, totally bounded metric space is compact, proved from countable choice used exactly once) is not.\n\nThe hypothesis $n \\ge 1$ is inherited from $\\mathbb{R}^n$ as the set of functions $n \\to \\mathbb{R}$, and $d_1$, $d_2$, $d_\\infty$ are metrics on it, which defines\n$\\mathbb{R}^n$ and its metrics only there; the last remark below records what\nhappens at $n = 0$.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[F2] [F2] [[thm-heine-borel-rn]]: closed bounded coordinate disks and half-disks in $\\mathbb R^2$ are compact.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Published result; complete current Statement and proof inspected for the scoped use.",
          "consumer_use": "[F2] [F2] [[thm-heine-borel-rn]]: closed bounded coordinate disks and half-disks in $\\mathbb R^2$ are compact."
        },
