      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "The chart formula for $d\\eta$ is invariant: under a smooth change $x=x(y)$, differentiating transformed coefficients creates symmetric second-derivative terms, which cancel after wedging. For every point of compact $D$, choose nested coordinate disks or half-disks with $\\overline{U_x}\\subset V_x$, $\\overline{V_x}\\subset W_x$, and $W_x$ contained in a chart inside the domain of $\\eta$; [F2] makes the closures compact. A finite family of the $U_x$ covers $D$. On their open union, a smooth partition subordinate to the corresponding $V_x$ exists by [F3]; each support lies in the compact closure of $V_x$, hence inside the larger chart $W_x$ away from its artificial edges. It decomposes $\\eta$ near $D$ into finitely many $C^1$ chart forms with compact support away from artificial chart edges. For an interior chart, extend every coefficient by zero to $\\mathbb R^2$. For a boundary chart with coordinates $(x,t)$, $t\\geq0$, extend each coefficient $f$ across $t=0$ by $\\widetilde f(x,t)=2f(x,0)-f(x,-t)$ for $t<0$ and $\\widetilde f(x,t)=f(x,t)$ for $t\\geq0$. The two formulas have the same value and first derivatives at $t=0$, so $\\widetilde f$ is $C^1$; compact chart support permits zero extension across artificial edges. Choose a nonnegative smooth mollifier $\\rho$ supported in the unit disk with integral one, and set $\\rho_\\epsilon(y)=\\epsilon^{-2}\\rho(y/\\epsilon)$. For each extension $f$ and $|\\alpha|\\leq1$, differentiation under the integral (or integration by parts) gives $\\partial^\\alpha(\\rho_\\epsilon*f)=\\rho_\\epsilon*(\\partial^\\alpha f)$, and $\\|\\rho_\\epsilon*(\\partial^\\alpha f)-\\partial^\\alpha f\\|_\\infty\\leq\\sup_{|y|\\leq\\epsilon}\\|\\partial^\\alpha f(\\,\\cdot-y)-\\partial^\\alpha f\\|_\\infty\\longrightarrow0,$ because the compactly supported coefficients and first derivatives are uniformly continuous. For sufficiently small $\\epsilon$, the convolved supports remain in their charts. Pulling back and summing the finite approximants gives smooth one-forms $\\eta_\\epsilon$ near $D$ with $\\eta_\\epsilon\\to\\eta$ in $C^1$.",
