        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Fix $\\epsilon$. Apply [F4] separately to the real and imaginary parts of $\\eta_\\epsilon$; adding the equalities gives Stokes for the complex form. Round each corner inside a coordinate disk or half-disk by replacing the two incident short arcs with a smooth joining arc. In its chart the changed boundary length is $O(r)$ and the changed area is $O(r^2)$ as the rounding radius $r\\downarrow0$; there are finitely many corners. Since $\\eta_\\epsilon$ and $d\\eta_\\epsilon$ are bounded on a compact neighborhood of $D$, the integrals over the changed pieces tend to zero. Apply [F4] to each rounded smooth region and pass to the corner limit; this proves the formula for $\\eta_\\epsilon$ on $D$. Finally, $C^1$ convergence gives uniform convergence of $d\\eta_\\epsilon$ on $D$ and of $\\eta_\\epsilon$ on its finitely many boundary arcs. Coordinate integration and [F5] let both integrals converge to those of $d\\eta$ and $\\eta$. Therefore the formula holds for $\\eta$.",
