          "carrier": "items/thm-nrqmx-berry-projector-curvature.md",
          "status": "Current Step 1 draft in the assigned batch; exact current carrier inspected.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $M$, $d$, and $P$ satisfy [[def-nrqmx-smooth-projector-bundle]], with constant rank $r$. The curvature of the projected connection $\\nabla=P\\,d$, as an endomorphism-valued two-form on $\\mathbb C^d$, is\n$$F=P(dP\\wedge dP)P.$$\nFor any local $C^2$ orthonormal frame $V:U\\to\\mathbb C^{d\\times r}$ with $V^*V=I_r$ and $P=VV^*$, its connection matrix $A=V^*dV$ is anti-Hermitian and its curvature matrix is $F_{\\mathrm{fr}}=dA+A\\wedge A=V^*FV$. If $V'=Vg$ for a $C^2$ map $g:U\\to U(r)$, then $A'=g^{-1}Ag+g^{-1}dg$ and $F'_{\\mathrm{fr}}=g^{-1}F_{\\mathrm{fr}}g$. Along every $C^2$ path, Kato transport is unitary and intertwines the endpoint projectors.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[F1] [F1] [[thm-nrqmx-berry-projector-curvature]]: a $C^2$ orthogonal projector has projected curvature $P(dP\\wedge dP)P$, and in a local unit frame its connection form is $u^*du$.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Current Step 1 draft; complete current proof inspected; independent acceptance is not inferred.",
          "consumer_use": "[F1] [F1] [[thm-nrqmx-berry-projector-curvature]]: a $C^2$ orthogonal projector has projected curvature $P(dP\\wedge dP)P$, and in a local unit frame its connection form is $u^*du$."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "lem-nrqmx-c2-projector-chern-integrality",
          "carrier": "items/lem-nrqmx-c2-projector-chern-integrality.md",
          "status": "Current Step 1 draft in the assigned batch; exact current carrier inspected.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Assume the full Axiom of Choice. Let $\\Sigma$ be a compact boundaryless oriented smooth surface, let $d\\geq1$, and let $P:\\Sigma\\to\\operatorname{End}(\\mathbb C^d)$ be a $C^2$ family of rank-one orthogonal projections. Let $E_P\\to\\Sigma$ be the complex line bundle with fibers $\\operatorname{ran}P(x)$, let $\\nabla=P\\,d$, and let $F=P(dP\\wedge dP)P$ be the curvature of this projected connection. Then\n$$\\frac{i}{2\\pi}\\int_\\Sigma\\operatorname{tr}F=\\langle c_1(E_P),[\\Sigma]\\rangle\\in\\mathbb Z.$$\nHere $c_1(E_P)$ is the first integral Chern class of the underlying topological complex line bundle, and $[\\Sigma]$ is the fundamental class for the stated orientation.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[F2] [F2] [[lem-nrqmx-c2-projector-chern-integrality]]: under full AC, for every $C^2$ rank-one projector on a compact boundaryless oriented smooth surface, $\\frac{i}{2\\pi}\\int\\operatorname{tr}F$ is an integer.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Current Step 1 draft; complete current proof inspected; independent acceptance is not inferred.",
          "consumer_use": "[F2] [F2] [[lem-nrqmx-c2-projector-chern-integrality]]: under full AC, for every $C^2$ rank-one projector on a compact boundaryless oriented smooth surface, $\\frac{i}{2\\pi}\\int\\operatorname{tr}F$ is an integer."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-c-r-and-smooth-maps-between-smooth-manifolds",
          "carrier": "items/def-c-r-and-smooth-maps-between-smooth-manifolds.md",
          "status": "Published mathematical supplier; exact current Definition inspected.",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Let $M$ and $N$ be smooth manifolds, let $F:M\\to N$ be a map, let $p\\in M$ and\nlet $r\\in\\mathbb N_0\\cup\\{\\infty\\}$, where $\\mathbb N_0=\\{0,1,2,\\ldots\\}$.\nFor $m,q\\in\\mathbb N_0$ and an open set $E\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$, a map\n$g:E\\to\\mathbb R^q$ is **of class $C^r$** as follows. When $m=0$, every such\nmap is declared $C^r$. When $m\\ge1$, each component $g_l$ with $l<q$ must be\n$C^r$ in the sense of\n[[def-ck-and-multi-index-notation-in-several-variables]]; when $q=0$ this\ncomponent condition is vacuous.\nSuppose $F$ is continuous at $p$. Then $F$ is **of class $C^r$ at $p$** when\nthere are smooth charts $(U,\\varphi)$ of $M$ at $p$ and $(V,\\psi)$ of $N$ at\n$F(p)$ with $F(U)\\subseteq V$ such that the coordinate representative\n\n$$\\psi\\circ F\\circ\\varphi^{-1}:\\varphi(U)\\longrightarrow\\psi(V)$$\n\n([[def-coordinate-representation-of-a-map]]) is of class $C^r$ on a\nneighbourhood of $\\varphi(p)$ in this componentwise Euclidean sense. By\n[[lem-chart-independence-of-c-r-smoothness]], this condition is independent of\nthe chosen charts: if one such representative is $C^r$, then every one is, so\n\"some charts\" may be read as \"any charts\". A map is **$C^r$ on an open set**\n$W\\subseteq M$ when it is continuous on $W$ and $C^r$ at every point of $W$. A\nmap that is $C^r$ for every finite $r$ — equivalently $C^\\infty$ — is called\n**smooth**; the term **$C^r$ map between smooth manifolds** is reserved for the\ncase where $F$ is continuous and the representative condition holds at every\npoint of $M$.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[F3] [F3] [[def-c-r-and-smooth-maps-between-smooth-manifolds]] and [[def-smooth-manifold]]: a map whose chart representatives have continuous derivatives through order two is $C^2$, and a smooth atlas is a compatible family of local charts.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Definition item; no proof is claimed or required for the defining interface.",
          "consumer_use": "[F3] [F3] [[def-c-r-and-smooth-maps-between-smooth-manifolds]] and [[def-smooth-manifold]]: a map whose chart representatives have continuous derivatives through order two is $C^2$, and a smooth atlas is a compatible family of local charts."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-smooth-manifold",
          "carrier": "items/def-smooth-manifold.md",
          "status": "Published mathematical supplier; exact current Definition inspected.",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "A **smooth $n$-manifold** is a pair $(M,\\mathcal S)$ in which $M$ is a\ntopological $n$-manifold\n([[def-topological-manifold-without-boundary]]) and $\\mathcal S$ is a **smooth\nstructure** on $M$: a maximal smooth atlas\n([[thm-each-smooth-atlas-is-contained-in-a-unique-maximal-smooth-atlas]]).\nBecause\n[[thm-each-smooth-atlas-is-contained-in-a-unique-maximal-smooth-atlas]] sends\nevery smooth atlas to the unique maximal atlas containing it, a smooth manifold\nis equivalently specified by a topological manifold $M$ together with any one\nsmooth atlas $\\mathcal A$, the structure being the generated $[\\mathcal A]$. A\nchart $(U,\\varphi)\\in\\mathcal S$ is called a **smooth chart** (or a **chart of\nthe smooth structure**); its domain is a **coordinate domain** and its\ncoordinate functions are **smooth coordinates** on $U$. When the structure is\nclear from context, the manifold itself is written $M$ in place of\n$(M,\\mathcal S)$.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[F3] [F3] [[def-c-r-and-smooth-maps-between-smooth-manifolds]] and [[def-smooth-manifold]]: a map whose chart representatives have continuous derivatives through order two is $C^2$, and a smooth atlas is a compatible family of local charts.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Definition item; no proof is claimed or required for the defining interface.",
          "consumer_use": "[F3] [F3] [[def-c-r-and-smooth-maps-between-smooth-manifolds]] and [[def-smooth-manifold]]: a map whose chart representatives have continuous derivatives through order two is $C^2$, and a smooth atlas is a compatible family of local charts."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-finite-dimensional-vector-valued-forms-and-their-exterior-derivative",
          "carrier": "items/def-finite-dimensional-vector-valued-forms-and-their-exterior-derivative.md",
          "status": "Published mathematical supplier; exact current Definition inspected.",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Let $M$ be a smooth manifold, let $V$ be a finite-dimensional real vector\nspace, and let $k\\geq0$. A **smooth $V$-valued differential $k$-form** on\n$M$ is a family of alternating $k$-linear maps\n\n$$\\alpha_p:(T_pM)^k\\longrightarrow V$$\n\nsuch that $\\lambda\\circ\\alpha$ is a scalar smooth $k$-form in the sense of\n[[def-smooth-differential-k-form]] for every $\\lambda\\in V^*$. Equivalently,\nfor one (and hence every) basis $(v_1,\\ldots,v_r)$ of $V$, the unique\ncomponents in $\\alpha=\\sum_i v_i\\alpha^i$ all belong to $\\Omega^k(M)$.\n\nFor such an $\\alpha$, its **componentwise exterior derivative** is the unique\n$V$-valued $(k+1)$-form $d_V\\alpha$ characterized by\n\n$$\\lambda\\circ d_V\\alpha=d(\\lambda\\circ\\alpha)$$\n\nfor every $\\lambda\\in V^*$. Indeed, in a basis with dual basis\n$(\\lambda^1,\\ldots,\\lambda^r)$, set\n\n$$d_V\\alpha=\\sum_i v_i\\,d\\alpha^i,\\qquad \\alpha^i=\\lambda^i\\circ\\alpha.$$\n\nThe scalar formula defining $d$ is real-linear term by term\n[[def-exterior-derivative-by-the-invariant-vector-field-formula]], and its\noutput is a smooth form\n[[lem-the-invariant-exterior-derivative-formula-is-c-infinity-multilinear]].\nThus the displayed construction has the stated characterization. It is\nindependent of the chosen basis because linear functionals separate points of\n$V$, and the characterization also proves uniqueness.\n\nThe two smoothness descriptions are equivalent in both directions: testing\nall $\\lambda$ includes the dual basis components, while every\n$\\lambda\\circ\\alpha$ is a fixed real linear combination of the components in\none basis. If $M$ is empty, these assignments and identities are vacuous. If\n$V=0$, there is only the zero-valued form and its derivative is zero; if\n$\\dim V=1$, the definition is exactly the scalar definition after choosing\none nonzero basis vector. No metric, nondegeneracy, manifold boundary, or\nendpoint is involved. A single finite basis exists by finite-dimensionality;\nthe definition is basis-independent and chooses no basis or family, so no\nchoice axiom is used.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[F4] [F4] [[def-finite-dimensional-vector-valued-forms-and-their-exterior-derivative]] and [[def-wedge-product-of-differential-forms]]: matrix-valued exterior derivatives are componentwise, and the wedge product is pointwise alternating multiplication; pullback commutes with these operations by the coordinate chain rule.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Definition item; no proof is claimed or required for the defining interface.",
          "consumer_use": "[F4] [F4] [[def-finite-dimensional-vector-valued-forms-and-their-exterior-derivative]] and [[def-wedge-product-of-differential-forms]]: matrix-valued exterior derivatives are componentwise, and the wedge product is pointwise alternating multiplication; pullback commutes with these operations by the coordinate chain rule."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "def-wedge-product-of-differential-forms",
          "carrier": "items/def-wedge-product-of-differential-forms.md",
          "status": "Published mathematical supplier; exact current Definition inspected.",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "exact_statement": "Let $\\alpha\\in\\Omega^k(M)$ and $\\beta\\in\\Omega^\\ell(M)$. Their **wedge product**\nis the differential form defined pointwise by\n\n$$ (\\alpha\\wedge\\beta)_p:=\\alpha_p\\wedge\\beta_p\\in\\operatorname{Alt}^{k+\\ell}(T_pM). $$\n\nThe next proposition verifies that this pointwise field is smooth.",
          "relevant_hypotheses_and_conventions": "[F4] [F4] [[def-finite-dimensional-vector-valued-forms-and-their-exterior-derivative]] and [[def-wedge-product-of-differential-forms]]: matrix-valued exterior derivatives are componentwise, and the wedge product is pointwise alternating multiplication; pullback commutes with these operations by the coordinate chain rule.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Definition item; no proof is claimed or required for the defining interface.",
          "consumer_use": "[F4] [F4] [[def-finite-dimensional-vector-valued-forms-and-their-exterior-derivative]] and [[def-wedge-product-of-differential-forms]]: matrix-valued exterior derivatives are componentwise, and the wedge product is pointwise alternating multiplication; pullback commutes with these operations by the coordinate chain rule."
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Write $n=(\\sin\\theta\\cos\\phi,\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi,\\cos\\theta)$ and use\n$$\\sigma_x=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix},\\quad\\sigma_y=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\\\i&0\\end{pmatrix},\\quad\\sigma_z=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&-1\\end{pmatrix}.$$\nDirect multiplication of the displayed matrices gives $\\sigma_j^2=I$ and $\\sigma_j\\sigma_k+\\sigma_k\\sigma_j=0$ for $j\\ne k$, while each $\\sigma_j$ has trace zero. Hence $(n\\cdot\\sigma)^2=(n_x^2+n_y^2+n_z^2)I=I$ and $\\operatorname{tr}(n\\cdot\\sigma)=0$, so $P_+$ is a rank-one orthogonal projector. On the northern chart, $u_N=(\\cos(\\theta/2),e^{i\\phi}\\sin(\\theta/2))^T$ is a unit frame and $A_N=u_N^*du_N=i(1-\\cos\\theta)d\\phi/2$, giving $dA_N=(i/2)\\omega$. On the southern chart $u_S=e^{-i\\phi}u_N$ is regular, and $A_S=A_N-i\\,d\\phi=-i(1+\\cos\\theta)d\\phi/2$, so $dA_S=(i/2)\\omega$ there as well. By [F1], these local calculations identify the global curvature trace as $\\operatorname{tr}F_+=(i/2)\\omega$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "Give each disk a smooth collar coordinate $(q,t)$, where $t\\geq0$ is inward distance from its boundary. Define $h(s)=e^{-1/s}$ for $s>0$ and $h(s)=0$ for $s\\leq0$. For $0\\leq t\\leq\\epsilon/2$ set $\\chi_0(t)=h(t)/(h(t)+h(\\epsilon/2-t))$, and for $t\\geq\\epsilon/2$ set $\\chi_0(t)=1$. Every derivative of $h$ vanishes at $0$, so $\\chi_0$ is smooth, flat at $0$, positive for $t>0$, at most $1$, and equal to $1$ near $\\epsilon$; hence $\\int_0^\\epsilon\\chi_0(t)\\,dt<\\epsilon$. Put $b(t)=h(t-\\epsilon/4)h(3\\epsilon/4-t)$, a nonnegative smooth function supported in $[\\epsilon/4,3\\epsilon/4]$ with positive integral, and define $\\chi=\\chi_0+c b$ where $c=(\\epsilon-\\int_0^\\epsilon\\chi_0)/\\int_0^\\epsilon b>0$. Then $\\chi$ is positive for $t>0$, flat at $0$, equals $1$ near $\\epsilon$, and has integral $\\epsilon$. Set $\\rho(t)=\\int_0^t\\chi(s)\\,ds$. Thus $\\rho(0)=\\rho'(0)=\\rho''(0)=0$, $\\rho'(t)>0$ for $t>0$, and $\\rho(t)=t$ near $\\epsilon$. Replace $f_j(q,t)$ by $f_j(q,\\rho(t))$ in each collar. This preserves area integral: the pullback coefficient is multiplied by $\\rho'(t)$, and substitution $s=\\rho(t)$ gives the original integral. The modified maps have equal boundary values and all derivatives through order two that contain a normal derivative vanish at the seam; their tangential derivatives through order two agree because the boundary traces agree. The collars therefore give compatible smooth seam charts on the compact boundaryless double of the disk, and the maps glue to a $C^2$ map $g$. The reversed orientation on the second disk gives $\\int g^*\\omega=\\int f_0^*\\omega-\\int f_1^*\\omega$.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
